1
00:01:00,935 --> 00:01:06,441
Fear. Treachery. Bloodlust.

2
00:01:06,608 --> 00:01:08,443
Thousands of years ago,

3
00:01:08,526 --> 00:01:11,863
these were the forces that ruled our world.

4
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:14,949
A world where prey were scared of predators.

5
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:20,288
And predators had an uncontrollable,
biological urge...

6
00:01:20,455 --> 00:01:22,624
to maim, and maul, and...

7
00:01:25,210 --> 00:01:27,587
Blood! Blood! Blood!

8
00:01:34,594 --> 00:01:36,805
And death.

9
00:01:43,228 --> 00:01:45,855
Back then, the world was divided in two.

10
00:01:46,022 --> 00:01:47,023
Vicious predator...

11
00:01:47,982 --> 00:01:49,609
or meek prey.

12
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:56,074
But over time, we evolved.

13
00:01:56,241 --> 00:01:59,869
And moved beyond our primitive, savage ways.

14
00:02:00,036 --> 00:02:03,915
Now, predator and prey live in harmony.

15
00:02:04,708 --> 00:02:08,962
And every young mammal has
multitudinous opportunities.

16
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:10,088
Yeah.

17
00:02:10,338 --> 00:02:13,591
I don't have to cower in a herd anymore.

18
00:02:13,758 --> 00:02:17,178
Instead, I can be an astronaut.

19
00:02:19,347 --> 00:02:22,517
I don't have to be a lonely hunter anymore.

20
00:02:22,684 --> 00:02:25,061
Today, I can hunt for tax exemptions.

21
00:02:25,228 --> 00:02:27,939
I'm gonna be an actuary!

22
00:02:29,441 --> 00:02:32,360
And I can make the world a better place.

23
00:02:32,527 --> 00:02:34,112
I am going to be...

24
00:02:36,072 --> 00:02:37,949
a police officer!

25
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:44,789
Bunny cop? That is the most
stupidest thing I ever heard.

26
00:02:44,956 --> 00:02:47,625
It may seem impossible to small minds...

27
00:02:47,792 --> 00:02:50,462
I'm looking at you, Gideon Grey.

28
00:02:50,795 --> 00:02:53,465
But, just 211 miles away...

29
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:57,218
stands the great city of Zootopia!

30
00:02:57,385 --> 00:03:00,722
Where our ancestors
first joined together in peace...

31
00:03:00,889 --> 00:03:07,228
and declared that anyone can be anything!

32
00:03:08,938 --> 00:03:11,065
Thank you and good night!

33
00:03:11,524 --> 00:03:15,403
Judy, you ever wonder how your mom
and me got to be so darn happy?

34
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:16,446
Nope.

35
00:03:16,613 --> 00:03:19,240
Well, we gave up on our dreams
and we settled. Right, Bon?

36
00:03:19,449 --> 00:03:21,659
Oh, yes, that's right, Stu.
We settled hard.

37
00:03:21,826 --> 00:03:24,662
See, that's the beauty of complacency, Jude.

38
00:03:24,829 --> 00:03:27,165
If you don't try anything new,
you'll never fail.

39
00:03:27,332 --> 00:03:28,708
I like trying actually.

40
00:03:28,875 --> 00:03:30,335
What you father means, hon...

41
00:03:30,502 --> 00:03:32,003
is it's gonna be difficult,

42
00:03:32,170 --> 00:03:34,464
impossible even,
for you to become a police officer.

43
00:03:34,631 --> 00:03:37,175
Right. There's never been a bunny cop.
Bunnies don't do that.

44
00:03:37,342 --> 00:03:38,384
- Never.
- Never.

45
00:03:39,302 --> 00:03:41,387
Then, I guess I'll have to be the first one.

46
00:03:41,554 --> 00:03:46,184
Because I am gonna make the world
a better place.

47
00:03:46,351 --> 00:03:50,230
Or, heck, you know, you want to talk
about making the world a better place...

48
00:03:50,438 --> 00:03:52,148
no better way to do it than
becoming a carrot farmer.

49
00:03:52,315 --> 00:03:54,275
Yes! Your dad, me...

50
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:58,530
your 275 brothers and sisters.
We're changing the world!

51
00:03:58,696 --> 00:03:59,572
One carrot at a time.

52
00:03:59,739 --> 00:04:02,492
Amen to that.
Carrot farming is a noble profession.

53
00:04:03,409 --> 00:04:04,889
Just putting the seeds in the ground.

54
00:04:04,953 --> 00:04:07,539
At one with the soil.
Just getting covered in dirt.

55
00:04:07,705 --> 00:04:09,465
You get it, honey.
It's great to have dreams.

56
00:04:09,541 --> 00:04:12,418
Yeah. Just as long as you don't
believe in them too much.

57
00:04:14,087 --> 00:04:15,255
Where'd the heck she go?

58
00:04:16,297 --> 00:04:18,091
Give me your tickets right now...

59
00:04:18,258 --> 00:04:20,885
or I'm gonna kick your meek little sheep butt.

60
00:04:21,261 --> 00:04:22,679
Cut it out, Gideon!

61
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:25,265
What are you gonna do? Cry?

62
00:04:25,431 --> 00:04:26,231
Hey!

63
00:04:26,349 --> 00:04:28,393
You heard her. Cut it out.

64
00:04:28,601 --> 00:04:30,436
Nice costume, loser.

65
00:04:30,603 --> 00:04:32,522
What crazy world are you living in

66
00:04:32,605 --> 00:04:34,983
where you think a bunny could be a cop?

67
00:04:35,149 --> 00:04:37,318
Kindly return my friend's tickets.

68
00:04:37,485 --> 00:04:38,403
Come and get them.

69
00:04:38,570 --> 00:04:40,572
But watch out, because I'm a fox,

70
00:04:40,655 --> 00:04:42,782
and like you said
in your dumb little stage play...

71
00:04:42,949 --> 00:04:45,285
us predators used to eat prey.

72
00:04:45,493 --> 00:04:47,745
And that killer instinct
is still in our "Dunnah."

73
00:04:47,912 --> 00:04:50,039
I'm pretty much sure
it's pronounced "DNA."

74
00:04:50,206 --> 00:04:51,749
Don't tell me what I know, Travis.

75
00:04:51,916 --> 00:04:53,751
You don't scare me, Gideon.

76
00:04:55,795 --> 00:04:57,881
- You scared now?
- Look at her nose twitch!

77
00:04:58,006 --> 00:04:58,882
She is scared!

78
00:04:59,048 --> 00:05:00,842
Cry little baby bunny.

79
00:05:01,092 --> 00:05:01,892
Cry, Cry...

80
00:05:05,305 --> 00:05:07,557
You don't know when to quit, do you?

81
00:05:14,856 --> 00:05:16,524
I want you to remember this moment...

82
00:05:16,691 --> 00:05:20,445
the next time you think you will ever
be anything more than just a stupid...

83
00:05:20,528 --> 00:05:22,530
carrot-farming, dumb bunny.

84
00:05:27,744 --> 00:05:28,745
That looks bad.

85
00:05:28,912 --> 00:05:30,413
Are you okay, Judy?

86
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:32,707
Yeah. I'm okay.

87
00:05:33,583 --> 00:05:34,584
Here you go.

88
00:05:34,751 --> 00:05:36,502
Wow! You got our tickets!

89
00:05:36,669 --> 00:05:38,046
You're awesome, Judy!

90
00:05:38,212 --> 00:05:41,591
Yeah, that Gideon Grey doesn't
know what he's talking about.

91
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:43,801
Well, he was right about one thing.

92
00:05:46,429 --> 00:05:49,557
I don't know when to quit.

93
00:05:51,142 --> 00:05:52,185
Listen up, cadets.

94
00:05:52,352 --> 00:05:56,356
Zootopia has 12 unique ecosystems
within its city limits.

95
00:05:56,648 --> 00:05:57,649
Tundratown...

96
00:05:57,857 --> 00:05:58,983
Sahara Square...

97
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:00,860
Rainforest District, to name a few.

98
00:06:01,361 --> 00:06:03,279
You're gonna have to master all of them

99
00:06:03,363 --> 00:06:05,782
before you hit the streets, or guess what?

100
00:06:06,115 --> 00:06:07,825
You'll be dead!

101
00:06:08,785 --> 00:06:10,912
Scorching sandstorm!

102
00:06:13,164 --> 00:06:14,666
You're dead, Bunny Bumpkin!

103
00:06:15,416 --> 00:06:17,669
1,000-foot fall!

104
00:06:18,711 --> 00:06:20,546
You're dead, Carrot Face!

105
00:06:21,005 --> 00:06:22,507
Frigid ice wall!

106
00:06:24,759 --> 00:06:26,010
You're dead, Farm Girl!

107
00:06:26,761 --> 00:06:28,680
Enormous criminal.

108
00:06:29,514 --> 00:06:32,100
You're dead.
Dead, dead, dead!

109
00:06:39,190 --> 00:06:42,402
Filthy toilet! You're dead, Fluff Butt.

110
00:06:42,860 --> 00:06:45,238
Just quit and go home, fuzzy bunny!

111
00:06:45,405 --> 00:06:46,645
There's never been a bunny cop.

112
00:06:46,739 --> 00:06:47,699
- Never.
- Never.

113
00:06:47,865 --> 00:06:50,535
Just a stupid,
carrot-farming dumb bunny.

114
00:07:17,603 --> 00:07:20,606
As mayor of Zootopia,
I am proud to announce...

115
00:07:20,815 --> 00:07:24,027
that my Mammal Inclusion Initiative
has produced...

116
00:07:24,235 --> 00:07:26,904
its first police academy graduate.

117
00:07:27,071 --> 00:07:32,410
Valedictorian of her class,
ZPD's very first rabbit officer...

118
00:07:32,577 --> 00:07:34,495
Judy Hopps.

119
00:07:34,662 --> 00:07:36,080
Oh, gosh.

120
00:07:38,750 --> 00:07:40,270
Assistant Mayor Bellwether, her badge.

121
00:07:40,334 --> 00:07:41,878
- Oh, yes. Right!
- Thank you.

122
00:07:42,045 --> 00:07:43,337
Yay, Judy!

123
00:07:43,504 --> 00:07:44,304
Judy...

124
00:07:44,756 --> 00:07:48,468
it is my great privilege
to officially assign you...

125
00:07:48,593 --> 00:07:51,763
to the heart of Zootopia: Precinct One.

126
00:07:51,929 --> 00:07:53,097
City Center.

127
00:07:54,891 --> 00:07:56,726
Congratulations, Officer Hopps.

128
00:07:57,185 --> 00:08:00,605
I won't let you down. This has been
my dream since I was a kid.

129
00:08:01,147 --> 00:08:03,357
It's a real proud day for us little guys.

130
00:08:03,483 --> 00:08:05,083
Bellwether, make room, will you?
Come on.

131
00:08:05,151 --> 00:08:07,612
Okay, Officer Hopps.
Let's see those teeth!

132
00:08:07,862 --> 00:08:09,447
- Officer Hopps, right here!
- Look this way please!

133
00:08:09,614 --> 00:08:10,698
Hold still. Smile!

134
00:08:13,451 --> 00:08:14,786
We're real proud of you, Judy.

135
00:08:14,952 --> 00:08:16,454
Yeah. Scared, too.

136
00:08:16,621 --> 00:08:18,873
Really, it's kind of
a proud-scared combo.

137
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:22,627
I mean, Zootopia. So far away.
Such a big city.

138
00:08:22,794 --> 00:08:23,628
Guys...

139
00:08:23,795 --> 00:08:25,838
I've been working for this my whole life.

140
00:08:26,005 --> 00:08:29,342
We know. And we're just
a little excited for you, but terrified.

141
00:08:29,801 --> 00:08:32,220
The only thing we have to fear is fear itself.

142
00:08:32,386 --> 00:08:34,847
And also bears.
We have bears to fear, too.

143
00:08:35,098 --> 00:08:36,378
Say nothing of lions and wolves.

144
00:08:36,557 --> 00:08:37,558
- Wolves?
- Weasels.

145
00:08:37,725 --> 00:08:38,726
You play cribbage with a weasel.

146
00:08:38,893 --> 00:08:40,019
And he cheats like there's no tomorrow.

147
00:08:40,353 --> 00:08:43,314
You know what, pretty much all
predators. And Zootopia's full of them.

148
00:08:43,481 --> 00:08:44,281
Oh, Stu.

149
00:08:44,315 --> 00:08:45,817
And foxes are the worst.

150
00:08:45,983 --> 00:08:50,071
Actually, your father does have
a point there. It's in their biology.

151
00:08:50,238 --> 00:08:51,531
Remember what happened with Gideon Grey?

152
00:08:51,697 --> 00:08:53,199
When I was nine.

153
00:08:53,324 --> 00:08:56,369
Gideon Grey was a jerk
who happened to be a fox.

154
00:08:56,494 --> 00:08:58,454
I know plenty of bunnies who are jerks.

155
00:08:58,579 --> 00:09:01,207
Sure, we all do. Absolutely.
But just in case...

156
00:09:01,415 --> 00:09:03,501
we made you a little care package
to take with you.

157
00:09:03,668 --> 00:09:05,711
- And I put some snacks in there.
- This is fox deterrent.

158
00:09:05,878 --> 00:09:06,921
Yeah, that's safe, to have that.

159
00:09:07,088 --> 00:09:08,506
This is fox repellent.

160
00:09:08,673 --> 00:09:09,632
The deterrent and the repellent,
that's all she needs.

161
00:09:09,757 --> 00:09:11,717
- Check this out!
- For goodness sake.

162
00:09:11,884 --> 00:09:13,678
She has no need for a fox Taser, Stu.

163
00:09:13,761 --> 00:09:16,013
Come on. When is there not
a need for a fox Taser?

164
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:18,933
Okay, look! I will take this,
to make you stop talking.

165
00:09:19,058 --> 00:09:20,726
Terrific! Everyone wins!

166
00:09:20,852 --> 00:09:23,437
Arriving, Zootopia Express.

167
00:09:25,857 --> 00:09:28,109
Okay, gotta go! Bye!

168
00:09:28,192 --> 00:09:29,986
Bye, Judy!

169
00:09:36,409 --> 00:09:38,119
I love you guys.

170
00:09:38,870 --> 00:09:40,121
Love you, too.

171
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:42,874
Oh, cripes, here come the waterworks.

172
00:09:43,374 --> 00:09:44,584
Oh, Stu, pull it together.

173
00:09:45,126 --> 00:09:46,460
Bye, everybody!

174
00:09:46,627 --> 00:09:47,795
- Bye-bye, Judy!
- Bye, Judy!

175
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:49,547
I love you!

176
00:09:49,964 --> 00:09:51,966
Bye! Bye!

177
00:09:55,052 --> 00:09:56,053
Good bye!

178
00:10:25,249 --> 00:10:29,253
I messed up tonight
I lost another fight

179
00:10:29,587 --> 00:10:33,090
Lost to myself
But I 'll just start again

180
00:10:33,382 --> 00:10:35,426
I keep falling down

181
00:10:35,551 --> 00:10:37,720
I keep on hitting the ground

182
00:10:37,845 --> 00:10:39,472
But I always get up now

183
00:10:39,639 --> 00:10:41,557
To see what's next

184
00:10:43,643 --> 00:10:45,770
Birds don't just fly

185
00:10:45,853 --> 00:10:47,813
They fall down and get up

186
00:10:51,525 --> 00:10:57,949
Nobody learns without getting it wrong

187
00:10:58,783 --> 00:11:02,078
I won't give up
No I won't give in

188
00:11:02,787 --> 00:11:06,207
Till I reach the end
And then I'll start again

189
00:11:06,290 --> 00:11:08,376
No I won't leave

190
00:11:08,459 --> 00:11:14,882
I want to try everything
I want to try even though I could fail

191
00:11:15,299 --> 00:11:18,719
I won't give up
No, I won't give in

192
00:11:18,803 --> 00:11:20,471
Till I reach the end

193
00:11:20,638 --> 00:11:22,974
Then I'll start again

194
00:11:23,182 --> 00:11:27,478
No, I won't leave
I want to try everything

195
00:11:27,645 --> 00:11:31,274
I want to try even though I could fail

196
00:11:35,444 --> 00:11:36,612
Try everything

197
00:11:39,615 --> 00:11:40,992
Try everything

198
00:11:43,995 --> 00:11:45,621
Try everything

199
00:11:49,667 --> 00:11:56,424
I'll keep on making those new mistakes

200
00:11:57,633 --> 00:12:04,348
And I'll keep on making them every day

201
00:12:05,683 --> 00:12:09,312
Those new mistakes

202
00:12:24,035 --> 00:12:25,202
Try everything

203
00:12:27,496 --> 00:12:29,206
Try everything

204
00:12:31,542 --> 00:12:33,294
Try everything

205
00:12:37,465 --> 00:12:41,469
Try everything

206
00:12:42,261 --> 00:12:45,973
I'm Gazelle. Welcome to Zootopia.

207
00:12:46,182 --> 00:12:48,559
Welcome to the Grand Pangolin Arms.

208
00:12:48,726 --> 00:12:51,520
Luxury apartments with charm.

209
00:12:51,687 --> 00:12:55,232
Complimentary delousing
once a month. Don't lose your key.

210
00:12:55,399 --> 00:12:56,734
Thank you.

211
00:12:56,901 --> 00:12:59,028
Hi! I'm Judy, your new neighbor.

212
00:12:59,195 --> 00:13:00,154
Yeah? Well, we're loud.

213
00:13:00,321 --> 00:13:02,490
Don't expect us to apologize for it.

214
00:13:05,618 --> 00:13:06,660
Greasy walls.

215
00:13:06,827 --> 00:13:08,120
Hey, shut up!

216
00:13:08,287 --> 00:13:09,205
Rickety bed.

217
00:13:09,372 --> 00:13:10,492
- You shut up!
- You shut up!

218
00:13:10,623 --> 00:13:11,540
Will you shut up?

219
00:13:12,083 --> 00:13:13,167
Crazy neighbors.

220
00:13:13,334 --> 00:13:14,418
I said, "Shut up!"

221
00:13:14,585 --> 00:13:15,920
I love it!

222
00:13:16,045 --> 00:13:17,254
Shut your mouth, shut up.

223
00:13:35,523 --> 00:13:36,440
Come on!

224
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:38,401
He bared his teeth first!

225
00:13:40,861 --> 00:13:41,946
Excuse me.

226
00:13:42,405 --> 00:13:43,572
Down here.

227
00:13:44,156 --> 00:13:45,032
Hi.

228
00:13:45,282 --> 00:13:47,118
O-M-Goodness!

229
00:13:47,284 --> 00:13:50,246
They really did hire a bunny! What!

230
00:13:50,413 --> 00:13:53,290
I gotta tell you, you are even cuter
than I thought you'd be!

231
00:13:54,125 --> 00:13:58,671
You probably didn't know, but a bunny
can call another bunny "cute"...

232
00:13:58,838 --> 00:14:00,423
but when other animals do it...

233
00:14:00,673 --> 00:14:01,632
it's a little...

234
00:14:02,758 --> 00:14:06,303
I am so sorry!
Me, Benjamin Clawhauser...

235
00:14:06,470 --> 00:14:10,641
the guy everyone thinks is just a flabby,
donut-loving cop, stereotyping you.

236
00:14:11,517 --> 00:14:15,354
No, it's okay. Oh, you've actually got...
There's a...

237
00:14:15,521 --> 00:14:16,814
In your neck. The fold.

238
00:14:16,981 --> 00:14:18,691
Where? Oh!

239
00:14:18,858 --> 00:14:21,694
There you went, you little dickens!

240
00:14:22,987 --> 00:14:24,822
I should get to roll call, which way do I...?

241
00:14:24,989 --> 00:14:26,532
Bullpen's over there to the left.

242
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:28,284
Great. Thank you.

243
00:14:29,702 --> 00:14:31,542
That poor little bunny's
gonna get eaten alive.

244
00:14:43,924 --> 00:14:45,885
Hey, Officer Hopps.

245
00:14:46,051 --> 00:14:47,887
You ready to make the world a better place?

246
00:14:52,433 --> 00:14:53,309
Atten-hut!

247
00:14:58,689 --> 00:15:01,192
All right! Everybody sit.

248
00:15:01,984 --> 00:15:04,653
I've got three items on the docket.

249
00:15:04,820 --> 00:15:09,533
First... we need to acknowledge
the elephant in the room.

250
00:15:10,242 --> 00:15:11,202
Francine...

251
00:15:12,244 --> 00:15:13,245
Happy birthday.

252
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:17,666
Number two.

253
00:15:17,833 --> 00:15:20,669
There are some new recruits
with us I should introduce...

254
00:15:20,836 --> 00:15:21,795
but I'm not going to...

255
00:15:21,962 --> 00:15:23,756
because I don't care.

256
00:15:25,925 --> 00:15:29,720
Finally, we have 14 missing mammal cases.

257
00:15:29,929 --> 00:15:33,724
All predators, from a giant polar bear
to a teensy little otter.

258
00:15:34,391 --> 00:15:37,978
And City Hall
is right up my tail to find them.

259
00:15:38,145 --> 00:15:41,398
This is priority number one.

260
00:15:41,565 --> 00:15:42,733
Assignments.

261
00:15:42,942 --> 00:15:44,193
Officers Grizzoli...

262
00:15:44,401 --> 00:15:45,402
Fangmeyer, Delgato.

263
00:15:45,486 --> 00:15:48,656
Your teams take missing mammals
from the Rainforest District.

264
00:15:49,281 --> 00:15:51,951
Officers McHorn, Rhinowitz, Wolfard.

265
00:15:52,117 --> 00:15:54,620
Your teams take Sahara Square.

266
00:15:54,912 --> 00:15:56,872
Officers Higgins, Snarlov, Trunkaby.

267
00:15:57,081 --> 00:15:58,958
Tundratown.

268
00:15:59,124 --> 00:16:01,919
And finally, our first bunny...

269
00:16:02,086 --> 00:16:04,630
Officer Hopps.

270
00:16:05,464 --> 00:16:06,465
Parking Duty.

271
00:16:07,049 --> 00:16:08,259
Dismissed.

272
00:16:08,425 --> 00:16:10,052
Parking duty?

273
00:16:11,303 --> 00:16:12,429
Chief!

274
00:16:12,638 --> 00:16:13,472
Chief Bogo?

275
00:16:14,932 --> 00:16:18,477
Sir, you said there were
14 missing mammal cases.

276
00:16:18,644 --> 00:16:20,145
- So?
- So, I can handle one.

277
00:16:20,312 --> 00:16:23,899
You probably forgot, but I was
top of my class at the Academy.

278
00:16:24,108 --> 00:16:26,485
Didn't forget. Just don't care.

279
00:16:26,735 --> 00:16:28,654
Sir, I'm not just some token bunny.

280
00:16:28,821 --> 00:16:31,657
Well, then writing 100 tickets a day
should be easy.

281
00:16:35,786 --> 00:16:38,581
100 tickets.
I'm not gonna write 100 tickets.

282
00:16:38,747 --> 00:16:40,833
I'm gonna write 200 tickets.

283
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,126
Before noon.

284
00:17:40,059 --> 00:17:41,018
Boom!

285
00:17:41,268 --> 00:17:42,895
200 tickets before noon!

286
00:17:48,233 --> 00:17:49,234
201.

287
00:17:51,111 --> 00:17:53,614
Hey, watch where you're going, fox!

288
00:18:20,599 --> 00:18:22,434
Where'd he go?

289
00:18:37,199 --> 00:18:40,994
I don't know what you're doing
skulking around during daylight hours...

290
00:18:41,203 --> 00:18:45,541
but I don't want any trouble in here.
So, hit the road.

291
00:18:45,749 --> 00:18:47,334
I'm not looking for any trouble either, sir.

292
00:18:47,418 --> 00:18:50,504
I simply want to buy a Jumbo Pop...

293
00:18:50,671 --> 00:18:52,172
for my little boy.

294
00:18:56,593 --> 00:18:58,971
You want the red or the blue, pal?

295
00:19:04,226 --> 00:19:05,686
I'm such a...

296
00:19:05,811 --> 00:19:06,729
Come on, kid. Back up.

297
00:19:06,895 --> 00:19:09,295
Listen, buddy. What? There aren't any
fox ice cream joints...

298
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:10,482
in your part of town?

299
00:19:10,649 --> 00:19:11,734
No, no. There are.

300
00:19:11,942 --> 00:19:15,696
There are. It's just, my boy,
this goofy little stinker...

301
00:19:15,863 --> 00:19:18,824
he loves all things elephant.
Wants to be one when he grows up.

302
00:19:19,658 --> 00:19:20,909
Is that adorable?

303
00:19:22,578 --> 00:19:25,622
Who the heck am I to crush
his little dreams? Right?

304
00:19:26,623 --> 00:19:28,625
You probably can't read, fox,
but the sign says...

305
00:19:28,792 --> 00:19:31,962
"We reserve the right to refuse service...

306
00:19:32,129 --> 00:19:34,339
"...to anyone."
So, beat it.

307
00:19:34,506 --> 00:19:36,508
You're holding up the line.

308
00:19:39,011 --> 00:19:41,346
Hello? Excuse me.

309
00:19:41,638 --> 00:19:44,475
You're gonna have to wait your turn
like everyone else, meter maid.

310
00:19:44,725 --> 00:19:46,435
Actually, I'm an officer.

311
00:19:46,643 --> 00:19:48,145
Just had a quick question.

312
00:19:48,353 --> 00:19:51,690
Are your customers aware
they're getting snot and mucus...

313
00:19:51,899 --> 00:19:53,059
with their cookies and cream?

314
00:19:54,735 --> 00:19:56,195
What are you talking about?

315
00:19:56,445 --> 00:19:59,865
I don't want to cause you any trouble,
but I believe scooping ice cream...

316
00:20:00,073 --> 00:20:02,576
with an ungloved trunk
is a Class 3 health code violation.

317
00:20:04,495 --> 00:20:06,330
Which is kind of a big deal.

318
00:20:06,497 --> 00:20:08,415
Of course,
I could let you off with a warning...

319
00:20:08,624 --> 00:20:12,211
if you were to glove those trunks and,
I don't know...

320
00:20:12,377 --> 00:20:15,714
finish selling this nice dad and his son a...

321
00:20:15,881 --> 00:20:16,840
What was it?

322
00:20:17,007 --> 00:20:19,092
A Jumbo Pop.
Please.

323
00:20:19,259 --> 00:20:20,677
A Jumbo Pop.

324
00:20:22,513 --> 00:20:24,056
$15.

325
00:20:24,306 --> 00:20:27,184
Thank you so much.
Thank you.

326
00:20:27,351 --> 00:20:30,604
Oh, no. Are you kidding me?
I don't have my wallet.

327
00:20:32,606 --> 00:20:35,567
I'd lose my head if it weren't attached
to my neck. That's the truth.

328
00:20:35,734 --> 00:20:39,530
Oh, boy. I'm sorry, pal.
Got to be about the worst birthday ever.

329
00:20:39,696 --> 00:20:41,865
Please don't be mad at me.

330
00:20:42,366 --> 00:20:43,700
Thanks anyway.

331
00:20:49,623 --> 00:20:51,250
Keep the change.

332
00:20:51,416 --> 00:20:53,377
Officer, I can't thank you enough.

333
00:20:53,544 --> 00:20:56,296
So kind, really. Can I pay you back?

334
00:20:56,463 --> 00:20:58,924
Oh, no, My treat.
It just...

335
00:20:59,091 --> 00:21:00,717
You know, it burns me up to see folks

336
00:21:00,801 --> 00:21:03,053
with such backward attitudes toward foxes.

337
00:21:03,262 --> 00:21:06,056
I just wanna say
you're a great dad and just a...

338
00:21:06,223 --> 00:21:09,101
a real articulate fella.

339
00:21:09,309 --> 00:21:13,814
Well, that is high praise. It's rare that
I find someone so non-patronizing.

340
00:21:13,981 --> 00:21:14,781
Officer...

341
00:21:14,898 --> 00:21:16,066
Hopps. Mister...

342
00:21:16,233 --> 00:21:18,569
Wilde. Nick Wilde.

343
00:21:18,735 --> 00:21:20,070
And you, little guy...

344
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:23,490
You want to be an elephant
when you grow up? You be an elephant.

345
00:21:23,657 --> 00:21:25,868
Because this is Zootopia.

346
00:21:26,034 --> 00:21:28,328
Anyone can be anything.

347
00:21:29,746 --> 00:21:31,106
Boy, I tell him that all the time.

348
00:21:31,123 --> 00:21:34,126
All right, here you go. Two paws. Yeah.

349
00:21:34,293 --> 00:21:37,254
Look at that smile.
That's a "happy birthday" smile.

350
00:21:37,421 --> 00:21:39,798
All right, give her a little
bye-bye toot-toot.

351
00:21:41,133 --> 00:21:42,342
Toot-toot!

352
00:21:42,509 --> 00:21:43,468
Bye, now.

353
00:21:43,635 --> 00:21:44,469
Good bye!

354
00:22:01,236 --> 00:22:02,946
Hey, little Toot-Toot...

355
00:22:50,535 --> 00:22:52,162
Popsicles!

356
00:22:52,329 --> 00:22:53,664
Get your popsicles!

357
00:23:18,981 --> 00:23:19,856
Lumber delivery.

358
00:23:20,023 --> 00:23:21,024
What's with the color?

359
00:23:21,191 --> 00:23:22,192
The color?

360
00:23:23,026 --> 00:23:24,111
That's red wood.

361
00:23:26,655 --> 00:23:28,115
39, 40. There you go.

362
00:23:28,865 --> 00:23:30,701
Way to work that diaper, big guy.

363
00:23:31,368 --> 00:23:33,829
Hey, no kiss bye-bye for daddy?

364
00:23:35,497 --> 00:23:38,709
You kiss me tomorrow,
I'll bite your face off.

365
00:23:39,876 --> 00:23:40,877
Ciao.

366
00:23:44,089 --> 00:23:47,426
Well. I stood up for you,
and you lied to me.

367
00:23:47,634 --> 00:23:48,844
You liar!

368
00:23:49,052 --> 00:23:50,554
It's called a hustle, sweetheart.

369
00:23:50,721 --> 00:23:52,723
And I'm not the liar. He is.

370
00:23:56,560 --> 00:23:57,394
Hey!

371
00:23:57,561 --> 00:24:00,397
All right, slick Nick, you're under arrest.

372
00:24:00,647 --> 00:24:01,898
Really? For what?

373
00:24:02,065 --> 00:24:04,443
Gee, I don't know.
How about selling food without a permit.

374
00:24:04,609 --> 00:24:06,729
Transporting undeclared commerce
across borough lines.

375
00:24:06,862 --> 00:24:07,904
False advertising.

376
00:24:08,071 --> 00:24:10,991
Permit. Receipt of declared commerce.

377
00:24:11,158 --> 00:24:13,410
And I didn't falsely advertise anything.
Take care.

378
00:24:13,577 --> 00:24:15,746
You told that mouse
the Popsicle sticks were redwood!

379
00:24:15,912 --> 00:24:18,415
That's right. "Red wood."
With a space in the middle.

380
00:24:18,582 --> 00:24:19,916
Wood that is red.

381
00:24:20,834 --> 00:24:24,379
You can't touch me, Carrots.
I've been doing this since I was born.

382
00:24:24,588 --> 00:24:26,298
You're gonna want to refrain
from calling me Carrots.

383
00:24:26,506 --> 00:24:27,841
My bad. I just naturally assumed

384
00:24:27,924 --> 00:24:30,260
you came from some little
carrot-choked Podunk, no?

385
00:24:30,469 --> 00:24:31,269
No!

386
00:24:31,386 --> 00:24:33,786
Podunk is in Deerbrooke County,
and I grew up in Bunnyburrow.

387
00:24:33,889 --> 00:24:37,392
Okay. Tell me
if this story sounds familiar.

388
00:24:37,559 --> 00:24:40,020
Naive little hick with good grades
and big ideas...

389
00:24:40,187 --> 00:24:42,773
decides, "Hey, look at me!
I'm gonna move to Zootopia...

390
00:24:42,939 --> 00:24:45,942
where predators and prey
live in harmony and sing "Kumbaya."

391
00:24:46,109 --> 00:24:48,153
Only to find, whoopsie...

392
00:24:48,361 --> 00:24:51,448
we don't all get along. And that
dream of becoming a big city cop?

393
00:24:51,615 --> 00:24:55,118
Double whoopsie. She's a meter maid.
And, whoopsie number three-sie...

394
00:24:55,285 --> 00:24:57,370
no one cares about her or her dreams.

395
00:24:57,537 --> 00:24:59,581
And soon enough, those dreams die

396
00:24:59,664 --> 00:25:01,458
and our bunny sinks into emotional
and literal squalor...

397
00:25:01,625 --> 00:25:03,126
living in a box under a bridge...

398
00:25:03,293 --> 00:25:05,629
till finally she has no choice
but to go back home...

399
00:25:05,796 --> 00:25:08,298
with that cute, fuzzy-wuzzy little tail
between her legs...

400
00:25:08,507 --> 00:25:09,382
to become...

401
00:25:09,549 --> 00:25:11,093
You're from Bunnyburrow,
is that what you said?

402
00:25:11,259 --> 00:25:14,137
So how about a carrot farmer.
That sound about right?

403
00:25:18,141 --> 00:25:21,353
Be careful, now, or it won't just
be your dreams getting crushed.

404
00:25:21,561 --> 00:25:25,649
Hey! No one tells me
what I can or can't be!

405
00:25:25,816 --> 00:25:27,818
Especially not some jerk...

406
00:25:27,984 --> 00:25:32,489
who never had the guts to try to be
anything more than a Popsicle hustler.

407
00:25:32,656 --> 00:25:36,159
All right, look.
Everyone comes to Zootopia...

408
00:25:36,368 --> 00:25:38,120
thinking they can be anything they want.

409
00:25:38,286 --> 00:25:39,663
Well, you can't.

410
00:25:39,830 --> 00:25:42,082
You can only be what you are.

411
00:25:42,290 --> 00:25:44,417
Sly fox, dumb bunny.

412
00:25:44,626 --> 00:25:46,419
I'm not a dumb bunny.

413
00:25:47,295 --> 00:25:49,881
Right. And that's not wet cement.

414
00:25:51,675 --> 00:25:52,801
You'll never be a real cop.

415
00:25:53,969 --> 00:25:55,289
You're a cute meter maid, though.

416
00:25:56,012 --> 00:25:57,722
Maybe a supervisor one day.

417
00:25:57,889 --> 00:25:59,808
Hang in there.

418
00:26:21,621 --> 00:26:23,874
You can't do nothing right, babe

419
00:26:24,040 --> 00:26:26,042
I'm a loser

420
00:26:52,360 --> 00:26:54,237
Oh, hey, it's my parents.

421
00:26:54,404 --> 00:26:55,906
Oh, there she is!

422
00:26:56,072 --> 00:26:56,872
Hi, sweetheart!

423
00:26:56,990 --> 00:26:59,743
Hey there, Jude the dude.
How was your first day on the force?

424
00:26:59,910 --> 00:27:01,912
- It was real great.
- Yeah?

425
00:27:02,078 --> 00:27:03,288
Everything you ever hoped?

426
00:27:03,496 --> 00:27:04,748
Absolutely.

427
00:27:04,915 --> 00:27:09,377
And more. Everyone's so nice and...

428
00:27:09,502 --> 00:27:10,921
- I feel like I'm making a difference.
- Wait a second.

429
00:27:11,087 --> 00:27:12,447
Holy cripes, Bonnie, look at that.

430
00:27:12,505 --> 00:27:14,049
Oh, my sweet heaven!

431
00:27:14,174 --> 00:27:16,218
Judy, are you a meter maid?

432
00:27:16,426 --> 00:27:18,803
This? No! Oh, no.
This is just a temporary thing.

433
00:27:18,970 --> 00:27:20,290
It's the safest job on the force!

434
00:27:20,430 --> 00:27:23,058
She's not a real cop.
Our prayers have been answered!

435
00:27:23,225 --> 00:27:25,435
- Glorious day!
- Meter maid! Meter maid!

436
00:27:25,602 --> 00:27:28,271
- Meter maid! Meter maid!
- Dad.Dad.Dad!

437
00:27:28,438 --> 00:27:30,106
It's been a really long clay.
I should really...

438
00:27:30,273 --> 00:27:31,553
That's right, you get some rest.

439
00:27:31,566 --> 00:27:32,901
Those meters aren't gonna maid themselves.

440
00:27:33,068 --> 00:27:34,444
Bye-bye.

441
00:27:35,111 --> 00:27:36,780
Buh-bye.

442
00:27:40,367 --> 00:27:42,953
Hey, buddy, turn down that depressing music.

443
00:27:44,454 --> 00:27:47,290
Leave the meter maid alone.
Didn't you hear her conversation?

444
00:27:47,457 --> 00:27:48,583
She feels like a failure!

445
00:27:48,750 --> 00:27:50,293
- Oh, shut up!
- You shut up!

446
00:27:50,460 --> 00:27:52,712
- You shut up!
- You shut up!

447
00:27:52,879 --> 00:27:54,631
Tomorrow's another day.

448
00:27:54,798 --> 00:27:57,092
Yeah, but it might be worse!

449
00:27:59,552 --> 00:28:01,471
I was 30 seconds over!

450
00:28:03,098 --> 00:28:05,100
You're a real hero, lady!

451
00:28:06,643 --> 00:28:08,979
My mommy says she wishes you were dead.

452
00:28:09,771 --> 00:28:13,275
Uncool, rabbit.
My tax dollars pay your salary.

453
00:28:14,317 --> 00:28:18,655
I am a real cop. I am a real cop.
I am a real cop.

454
00:28:18,822 --> 00:28:21,408
Hey, hey! You, bunny!

455
00:28:21,574 --> 00:28:25,453
Sir. If you have a grievance, you may
contest your citation in traffic court.

456
00:28:25,620 --> 00:28:27,497
What are you talking about?
My shop!

457
00:28:27,664 --> 00:28:29,541
It was just robbed! Look!

458
00:28:29,708 --> 00:28:31,042
He's getting away!

459
00:28:31,167 --> 00:28:32,627
Are you a cop or not?

460
00:28:32,836 --> 00:28:35,588
Yes! Don't worry, sir!
I've got this!

461
00:28:40,385 --> 00:28:41,928
Stop! Stop, in the name of the law!

462
00:28:42,679 --> 00:28:44,931
Catch me if you can, Cottontail!

463
00:28:52,689 --> 00:28:53,489
Coming through!

464
00:28:53,606 --> 00:28:55,317
This is Officer McHorn, we got a 10-31.

465
00:28:55,483 --> 00:28:56,609
I got dibs!

466
00:28:56,776 --> 00:28:58,194
Officer Hopps. I am in pursuit!

467
00:29:14,961 --> 00:29:16,588
You!

468
00:29:16,755 --> 00:29:17,555
Freeze!

469
00:29:17,714 --> 00:29:19,883
Hey, meter maid! Wait for the real cops!

470
00:29:21,217 --> 00:29:22,218
Stop!

471
00:29:50,038 --> 00:29:51,039
Sorry. Coming through.

472
00:29:51,206 --> 00:29:53,458
Excuse me. Excuse me. Pardon.

473
00:29:58,213 --> 00:29:59,339
Bon voyage-e, flat foot!

474
00:30:16,940 --> 00:30:18,400
Hey, stop right there!

475
00:30:18,525 --> 00:30:19,859
Have a donut, copper!

476
00:30:21,694 --> 00:30:25,073
Oh, my God. Did you see
those leopard print jeggings?

477
00:30:31,287 --> 00:30:32,664
I love your hair.

478
00:30:33,206 --> 00:30:34,290
Thank you.

479
00:30:35,417 --> 00:30:37,127
Come to papa.

480
00:30:40,130 --> 00:30:42,799
You're gonna have to be patient and
wait in line just like everyone else...

481
00:30:42,966 --> 00:30:45,009
Mrs. Otterton. Okay?

482
00:30:51,808 --> 00:30:53,810
I popped the weasel!

483
00:30:53,977 --> 00:30:55,728
Hopps!

484
00:30:57,772 --> 00:30:59,399
Abandoning your post.

485
00:30:59,482 --> 00:31:00,900
Inciting a scurry.

486
00:31:01,067 --> 00:31:02,986
Reckless endangerment of rodents.

487
00:31:03,111 --> 00:31:04,988
But, to be fair...

488
00:31:05,155 --> 00:31:08,741
you did stop a master criminal
from stealing two dozen moldy onions.

489
00:31:09,659 --> 00:31:12,078
Hate to disagree with you, sir,
but those aren't onions.

490
00:31:12,203 --> 00:31:14,956
Those are a crocus varietal
called midnicampum holicithias.

491
00:31:15,081 --> 00:31:16,401
They're a Class-C botanical, sir.

492
00:31:16,499 --> 00:31:19,085
I grew up in a family where
plant husbandry was kind of a thing.

493
00:31:19,252 --> 00:31:20,753
Shut your tiny mouth now!

494
00:31:21,671 --> 00:31:23,423
Sir, I got the bad guy.

495
00:31:23,590 --> 00:31:24,632
That's my job.

496
00:31:24,799 --> 00:31:27,927
Your job is putting tickets on parked cars!

497
00:31:28,094 --> 00:31:30,847
Chief, Mrs. Otterton's here
to see you again.

498
00:31:31,014 --> 00:31:31,931
Not now.

499
00:31:32,098 --> 00:31:33,349
Okay, I just didn't know
if you'd want to take it this time.

500
00:31:33,516 --> 00:31:34,476
She seems really upset.

501
00:31:34,642 --> 00:31:36,060
- Not now!
- Sir...

502
00:31:36,269 --> 00:31:40,023
I don't want to be a meter maid,
I want to be a real cop.

503
00:31:40,190 --> 00:31:43,943
Do you think the mayor asked me what
I wanted when he assigned you to me?

504
00:31:45,195 --> 00:31:46,321
But, sir, if...

505
00:31:46,529 --> 00:31:49,199
Life isn't some cartoon musical
where you sing a little song...

506
00:31:49,365 --> 00:31:52,785
and your insipid dreams magically come true.

507
00:31:52,952 --> 00:31:55,705
So, let it go.

508
00:31:56,664 --> 00:31:59,834
Chief Bogo, please.
Five minutes of your time.

509
00:32:00,001 --> 00:32:01,044
I'm sorry, sir.

510
00:32:01,211 --> 00:32:03,338
I tried to stop her.
She is super-slippery.

511
00:32:03,505 --> 00:32:05,215
I gotta go sit down.

512
00:32:05,465 --> 00:32:08,718
Ma'am, as I've told you,
we're doing everything we can.

513
00:32:08,885 --> 00:32:12,680
My husband has been missing for
10 days. His name is Emmitt Otterton.

514
00:32:12,847 --> 00:32:13,890
Yes, I know.

515
00:32:14,057 --> 00:32:16,100
He's a florist.

516
00:32:16,267 --> 00:32:19,521
We have two beautiful children.
He would never just disappear.

517
00:32:19,771 --> 00:32:22,649
Ma'am, our detectives are very busy.

518
00:32:22,815 --> 00:32:26,861
Please. There's got to be someone
to find my Emmitt.

519
00:32:26,986 --> 00:32:27,820
Mrs. Otterton...

520
00:32:27,987 --> 00:32:28,947
I will find him.

521
00:32:30,740 --> 00:32:32,200
Thank you.

522
00:32:32,534 --> 00:32:35,411
Bless you. Bless you, little bunny.

523
00:32:38,081 --> 00:32:39,123
Take this.

524
00:32:39,290 --> 00:32:41,668
Find my Emmitt. Bring him home...

525
00:32:41,834 --> 00:32:43,753
to me and my babies, please.

526
00:32:45,129 --> 00:32:46,256
Mrs. Otterton, please wait out here.

527
00:32:46,422 --> 00:32:48,091
Of course. Thank you both so much.

528
00:32:48,591 --> 00:32:49,551
One second.

529
00:32:51,094 --> 00:32:52,762
You're fired.

530
00:32:52,929 --> 00:32:54,097
What? Why?

531
00:32:54,264 --> 00:32:55,598
Insubordination!

532
00:32:55,765 --> 00:32:57,433
Now. I'm going to open this door...

533
00:32:57,600 --> 00:33:00,395
and you're going to tell that otter
you're a former meter maid...

534
00:33:00,645 --> 00:33:02,730
with delusions of grandeur...

535
00:33:02,939 --> 00:33:04,607
who will not be taking the case!

536
00:33:04,732 --> 00:33:07,610
I just heard Officer Hopps is taking the case.

537
00:33:07,777 --> 00:33:09,237
Assistant Mayor Bellwether.

538
00:33:09,362 --> 00:33:12,657
The Mammal Inclusion Initiative
is really starting to pay off.

539
00:33:12,824 --> 00:33:15,535
Mayor Lionheart is just gonna be so jazzed!

540
00:33:15,660 --> 00:33:16,619
No, let's not tell the Mayor just yet.

541
00:33:16,786 --> 00:33:19,622
And I've sent it and it is done,
so I did do that.

542
00:33:19,789 --> 00:33:21,389
Well, I'd say the case is in good hands.

543
00:33:21,457 --> 00:33:24,085
Us little guys really need to
stick together, right?

544
00:33:24,460 --> 00:33:25,545
Like glue.

545
00:33:26,087 --> 00:33:27,130
Good one.

546
00:33:27,297 --> 00:33:29,257
Just call me if you ever need anything, okay?

547
00:33:29,424 --> 00:33:31,264
You've always got a friend at City Hall, Judy.

548
00:33:31,342 --> 00:33:32,760
All right, bye-bye.

549
00:33:32,927 --> 00:33:34,971
Thank you, ma'am.

550
00:33:39,601 --> 00:33:42,145
I will give you 48 hours.

551
00:33:42,312 --> 00:33:43,396
Yes!

552
00:33:43,563 --> 00:33:44,814
That's two days to find Emmitt Otterton.

553
00:33:44,981 --> 00:33:45,607
Okay.

554
00:33:45,773 --> 00:33:49,986
But, you strike out, you resign.

555
00:33:54,157 --> 00:33:55,325
Okay.

556
00:33:55,491 --> 00:33:56,367
Deal!

557
00:33:56,534 --> 00:34:00,330
Splendid. Clawhauser will
give you the complete case file.

558
00:34:00,580 --> 00:34:01,664
Here you go.

559
00:34:01,789 --> 00:34:03,291
One missing otter.

560
00:34:04,876 --> 00:34:05,877
That's it?

561
00:34:06,044 --> 00:34:09,213
Yikes! That is the smallest case file
I've ever seen.

562
00:34:09,422 --> 00:34:11,633
Leads, none. Witnesses, none.

563
00:34:11,758 --> 00:34:14,844
And you're not in the computer system
yet, so resources, none!

564
00:34:15,845 --> 00:34:19,182
I hope you didn't stake your career
on cracking this one!

565
00:34:19,349 --> 00:34:22,602
Okay. Last known sighting.

566
00:34:26,731 --> 00:34:29,025
Can I just borrow...
Thank you.

567
00:34:32,028 --> 00:34:33,237
Popsicle?

568
00:34:33,363 --> 00:34:34,906
The murder weapon.

569
00:34:35,073 --> 00:34:36,574
Get your Popsicle.

570
00:34:36,741 --> 00:34:40,411
Yeah. Because that...
What does that mean?

571
00:34:40,578 --> 00:34:41,496
It means...

572
00:34:42,372 --> 00:34:44,832
I have a lead.

573
00:34:50,213 --> 00:34:52,882
Hi! Hello? It's me, again!

574
00:34:53,049 --> 00:34:55,051
Hey, it's Officer Toot-Toot.

575
00:34:56,177 --> 00:34:58,221
No. Actually, it's Officer Hopps...

576
00:34:58,346 --> 00:35:00,386
and I'm here to ask you some questions
about a case.

577
00:35:00,390 --> 00:35:02,850
What happened, meter maid?
Did someone steal a traffic cone?

578
00:35:03,017 --> 00:35:04,727
It wasn't me.

579
00:35:07,605 --> 00:35:10,233
Hey, Carrots, you're going to
wake the baby. I gotta get to work.

580
00:35:10,525 --> 00:35:11,609
This is important, sir.

581
00:35:11,776 --> 00:35:14,904
I think your $10 worth of popsicles can wait.

582
00:35:15,697 --> 00:35:18,282
I make 200 bucks a day, Fluff!

583
00:35:18,408 --> 00:35:21,619
365 days a year, since I was 12.

584
00:35:21,786 --> 00:35:23,371
And time is money. Hop along.

585
00:35:23,538 --> 00:35:25,456
Please, just look at the picture.

586
00:35:25,665 --> 00:35:28,209
You sold Mr. Otterton that Popsicle,
right? Do you know him?

587
00:35:28,334 --> 00:35:29,134
I know everybody.

588
00:35:29,711 --> 00:35:31,796
And I also know that, somewhere...

589
00:35:31,963 --> 00:35:33,089
there's a toy store
missing its stuffed animal,

590
00:35:33,172 --> 00:35:35,883
so why don't you get back to your box?

591
00:35:36,050 --> 00:35:37,850
Fine. Then we'll have to do this
the hard way.

592
00:35:39,595 --> 00:35:41,139
Did you just boot my stroller?

593
00:35:41,305 --> 00:35:43,141
Nicholas Wilde, you are under arrest.

594
00:35:43,975 --> 00:35:45,935
For what? Hurting your feelings?

595
00:35:46,102 --> 00:35:47,270
Felony Tax Evasion.

596
00:35:47,937 --> 00:35:53,276
Yeah, $200 a day, 365 days a year,
since you were 12.

597
00:35:53,443 --> 00:35:58,072
That's two decades, so times 20,
which is $1,460,000, I think.

598
00:35:58,239 --> 00:36:01,784
I mean,I am just a dumb bunny,
but we are good at multiplying.

599
00:36:01,951 --> 00:36:03,703
Anyway, according to your tax forms...

600
00:36:03,870 --> 00:36:06,581
you reported, let me see here... zero!

601
00:36:06,789 --> 00:36:11,002
Unfortunately, lying on a federal form
is a punishable offense.

602
00:36:11,169 --> 00:36:12,628
Five years jail time.

603
00:36:12,795 --> 00:36:15,006
Well, it's my word against yours.

604
00:36:15,965 --> 00:36:17,842
<i>200 bucks</i> a <i>day, Fluff!</i>

605
00:36:18,009 --> 00:36:20,678
365 days a year, since I was 12.

606
00:36:20,845 --> 00:36:22,722
Actually, it's your word against yours.

607
00:36:22,847 --> 00:36:26,267
And if you want this pen, you're going to
help me find this poor, missing otter...

608
00:36:26,434 --> 00:36:30,271
or the only place you'll be selling
popsicles is the prison cafeteria.

609
00:36:31,314 --> 00:36:33,149
It's called a hustle, sweetheart.

610
00:36:33,483 --> 00:36:35,485
She hustled you.

611
00:36:39,363 --> 00:36:42,241
She hustled you good!

612
00:36:42,450 --> 00:36:45,495
You a cop now, Nick.
You gonna need one of these.

613
00:36:46,662 --> 00:36:47,830
Have fun...

614
00:36:47,997 --> 00:36:49,040
working with the fuzz!

615
00:36:51,292 --> 00:36:52,710
Start talking.

616
00:36:53,377 --> 00:36:56,672
I don't know where he is.
I only saw where he went.

617
00:36:56,923 --> 00:36:57,840
Great. Let's go.

618
00:36:59,217 --> 00:37:01,219
It's not exactly a place for...

619
00:37:01,427 --> 00:37:02,512
a cute little bunny.

620
00:37:02,678 --> 00:37:04,639
Don't call me cute. Get in the car.

621
00:37:04,764 --> 00:37:06,682
Okay. You're the boss.

622
00:37:25,743 --> 00:37:27,078
Hi.

623
00:37:27,203 --> 00:37:28,204
Hello?

624
00:37:30,331 --> 00:37:31,541
Hello?

625
00:37:31,749 --> 00:37:33,626
Hello? Hello?

626
00:37:36,671 --> 00:37:38,256
Hello. My name is...

627
00:37:38,464 --> 00:37:41,551
You know, I'm going to hit
the pause button right there...

628
00:37:41,717 --> 00:37:43,970
because we're all good on Bunny Scout cookies.

629
00:37:44,971 --> 00:37:46,013
No.

630
00:37:46,180 --> 00:37:48,432
I'm Officer Hopps, ZPD.

631
00:37:48,599 --> 00:37:52,353
I'm looking for a missing mammal,
Emmitt Otterton, right here...

632
00:37:52,520 --> 00:37:55,106
who may have frequented this establishment.

633
00:37:59,777 --> 00:38:01,362
Yeah, old Emmitt.

634
00:38:02,613 --> 00:38:04,907
Haven't seen him in a couple of weeks.

635
00:38:05,074 --> 00:38:08,244
But, hey, you should talk
to his yoga instructor.

636
00:38:08,411 --> 00:38:10,079
I'd be happy to take you back.

637
00:38:10,246 --> 00:38:11,122
Thank you so much.

638
00:38:11,289 --> 00:38:13,624
I'd appreciate that more than
you can imagine, it would be such...

639
00:38:13,708 --> 00:38:14,834
You are naked!

640
00:38:15,334 --> 00:38:18,087
For sure, we're a naturalist club.

641
00:38:18,254 --> 00:38:21,507
Yeah. In Zootopia,
anyone can be anything.

642
00:38:21,716 --> 00:38:23,551
These guys, they be naked.

643
00:38:23,718 --> 00:38:26,679
Nangi's just on the other side
of the Pleasure Pool.

644
00:38:34,145 --> 00:38:35,271
Oh, boy.

645
00:38:35,479 --> 00:38:37,273
Does this make you uncomfortable?

646
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:40,860
Because if so, there is no
shame in calling it quits.

647
00:38:41,027 --> 00:38:42,987
Yes, there is.

648
00:38:43,154 --> 00:38:45,156
Boy, that's the spirit.

649
00:38:46,365 --> 00:38:49,452
Yeah, some mammals say
the naturalist life is weird...

650
00:38:49,619 --> 00:38:51,704
but you know what I say is weird?

651
00:38:51,829 --> 00:38:53,706
Clothes on animals!

652
00:38:54,457 --> 00:38:56,125
Here we go.

653
00:38:56,292 --> 00:39:00,963
As you can see, Nangi's an elephant,
so she'll totally remember everything.

654
00:39:01,130 --> 00:39:02,298
Hey, Nangi.

655
00:39:02,465 --> 00:39:05,676
These dudes have some questions
about Emmitt the otter.

656
00:39:05,843 --> 00:39:06,643
Who?

657
00:39:06,802 --> 00:39:10,139
Emmitt Otterton? Been coming to
your yoga class for like...

658
00:39:10,306 --> 00:39:11,641
6 years?

659
00:39:11,849 --> 00:39:13,392
I have no memory of this beaver.

660
00:39:13,559 --> 00:39:15,311
He's an otter, actually.

661
00:39:15,436 --> 00:39:17,276
He was here a couple Wednesdays ago, remember?

662
00:39:17,355 --> 00:39:18,155
No.

663
00:39:18,230 --> 00:39:22,026
Yeah, he was wearing
a green cable-knit sweater vest...

664
00:39:22,151 --> 00:39:27,490
and a new pair of corduroy slacks.
And a paisley tie, sweet Windsor knot.

665
00:39:27,657 --> 00:39:29,533
Real tight. Remember that, Nangi?

666
00:39:29,700 --> 00:39:30,451
No.

667
00:39:30,493 --> 00:39:34,372
And we both walked him out,
and he got into this big old white car

668
00:39:34,455 --> 00:39:35,831
with a silver trim.

669
00:39:36,040 --> 00:39:38,793
Needed a tune-up.
The third cylinder wasn't firing.

670
00:39:38,960 --> 00:39:40,836
- Remember that, Nangi?
- No.

671
00:39:41,420 --> 00:39:45,758
You didn't happen to catch
the license plate number, did you?

672
00:39:45,967 --> 00:39:48,844
Oh, for sure. It was 2-9...

673
00:39:49,011 --> 00:39:50,763
T-H-D...

674
00:39:51,013 --> 00:39:52,807
- 0-3.
- 0-3. Wow.

675
00:39:52,932 --> 00:39:54,492
This is a lot of great info. Thank you.

676
00:39:54,517 --> 00:39:57,687
Told you Nangi has a mind like a steel trap.

677
00:39:57,853 --> 00:40:01,899
I wish I had a memory like an elephant.

678
00:40:03,109 --> 00:40:05,236
Well, I had a ball.

679
00:40:05,403 --> 00:40:06,862
You are welcome for the clue...

680
00:40:07,029 --> 00:40:08,906
and seeing as how any moron can run a plate,

681
00:40:08,990 --> 00:40:11,909
I will take that pen and bid you adieu.

682
00:40:12,368 --> 00:40:14,870
The plate. I can't run a plate.

683
00:40:15,037 --> 00:40:16,330
I'm not in the system yet.

684
00:40:16,706 --> 00:40:18,582
Give me the pen, please.

685
00:40:18,791 --> 00:40:20,209
What was it you said?

686
00:40:20,376 --> 00:40:22,378
"Any moron can run a plate"?

687
00:40:22,545 --> 00:40:27,299
Gosh. If only there were a moron around
who were up to the task.

688
00:40:27,466 --> 00:40:30,720
Rabbit, I did what you asked!
You can't keep me on the hook forever.

689
00:40:30,886 --> 00:40:35,099
Not forever. Well, I only have
36 hours left to solve this case.

690
00:40:35,266 --> 00:40:37,685
So can you run the plate or not?

691
00:40:39,020 --> 00:40:41,480
Actually, I just remembered...

692
00:40:41,605 --> 00:40:42,940
I have a pal at the DMV.

693
00:40:44,233 --> 00:40:45,735
Flash is the fastest guy in there.

694
00:40:45,901 --> 00:40:47,570
If you need something done, he's on it.

695
00:40:47,695 --> 00:40:50,375
I hope so. We are really fighting
the clock and every minute counts.

696
00:40:51,073 --> 00:40:55,202
Wait. They're all sloths?

697
00:41:23,522 --> 00:41:25,066
You said this was going to be quick!

698
00:41:25,232 --> 00:41:28,360
Are you saying that because
he's a sloth, he can't be fast?

699
00:41:28,527 --> 00:41:30,946
I thought in Zootopia,
anyone could be anything.

700
00:41:31,405 --> 00:41:33,949
Flash, Flash, hundred-yard dash.
Buddy, it's nice to see you.

701
00:41:35,284 --> 00:41:37,078
Nice to...

702
00:41:37,953 --> 00:41:39,080
see you...

703
00:41:40,623 --> 00:41:41,624
too.

704
00:41:41,791 --> 00:41:44,085
Hey, Flash, I'd love you to meet my friend.

705
00:41:44,585 --> 00:41:46,170
Darling, I've forgotten your name.

706
00:41:46,921 --> 00:41:49,465
Officer Judy Hopps, ZPD, how are you?

707
00:41:49,632 --> 00:41:50,966
I am...

708
00:41:51,467 --> 00:41:52,468
doing...

709
00:41:52,968 --> 00:41:54,470
just...

710
00:41:54,845 --> 00:41:55,805
Fine?

711
00:41:55,971 --> 00:41:58,140
...as well as...

712
00:41:58,307 --> 00:41:59,517
I can...

713
00:42:00,017 --> 00:42:00,851
be.

714
00:42:01,018 --> 00:42:02,269
- What...
- Hang in there.

715
00:42:02,436 --> 00:42:03,479
...can I...

716
00:42:04,188 --> 00:42:05,908
- ...do...
- I was hoping you could run a...

717
00:42:05,940 --> 00:42:06,941
...for you...

718
00:42:07,108 --> 00:42:08,150
Well, I was hoping you could...

719
00:42:08,317 --> 00:42:09,360
...today?

720
00:42:12,113 --> 00:42:14,490
Well, I was hoping you could
run a plate for us.

721
00:42:14,657 --> 00:42:16,367
We are in a really big hurry.

722
00:42:18,035 --> 00:42:19,286
Sure.

723
00:42:19,453 --> 00:42:20,788
What's the...

724
00:42:21,330 --> 00:42:23,124
- plate...
- 2-9-T...

725
00:42:23,290 --> 00:42:24,416
...number?

726
00:42:25,292 --> 00:42:27,294
2-9-T-H-D-0-3

727
00:42:36,428 --> 00:42:37,513
Two...

728
00:42:39,807 --> 00:42:40,808
nine...

729
00:42:41,267 --> 00:42:43,310
T-H-D-0-3.

730
00:42:44,186 --> 00:42:45,354
...T...

731
00:42:46,147 --> 00:42:47,648
H-D-0-3.

732
00:42:48,524 --> 00:42:49,358
...H...

733
00:42:49,525 --> 00:42:51,527
D-0-3.

734
00:42:54,822 --> 00:42:55,698
...D...

735
00:42:55,865 --> 00:42:57,324
0-3.

736
00:42:58,826 --> 00:42:59,626
...0...

737
00:42:59,702 --> 00:43:00,786
3.

738
00:43:02,705 --> 00:43:04,206
Hey, Flash, want to hear a joke?

739
00:43:04,373 --> 00:43:05,374
No!

740
00:43:05,833 --> 00:43:06,834
Sure.

741
00:43:07,293 --> 00:43:11,380
Okay. What do you call
a three-humped camel?

742
00:43:12,381 --> 00:43:13,799
I don't...

743
00:43:13,966 --> 00:43:15,176
know.

744
00:43:15,342 --> 00:43:16,385
What...

745
00:43:16,510 --> 00:43:17,553
do...

746
00:43:17,720 --> 00:43:19,388
you call...

747
00:43:19,555 --> 00:43:21,473
- a...
- Three-humped camel.

748
00:43:21,640 --> 00:43:23,058
...three-humped...

749
00:43:23,225 --> 00:43:24,059
camel?

750
00:43:25,227 --> 00:43:26,228
Pregnant.

751
00:43:41,785 --> 00:43:43,162
Yes, very funny, very funny.

752
00:43:43,329 --> 00:43:44,689
Can we please just focus on the...

753
00:43:44,747 --> 00:43:46,207
- Hey...
- Wait, wait, wait!

754
00:43:46,290 --> 00:43:47,583
...Priscilla!

755
00:43:47,750 --> 00:43:48,918
Oh, no!

756
00:43:49,752 --> 00:43:51,420
Yes...

757
00:43:51,587 --> 00:43:53,255
Flash?

758
00:43:53,339 --> 00:43:54,139
What...

759
00:43:54,256 --> 00:43:55,090
do...

760
00:43:55,257 --> 00:43:56,926
- No!
- ...you call a...

761
00:43:57,092 --> 00:43:59,511
A three-humped camel? "Pregnant!"
Okay, great, we got it.

762
00:43:59,678 --> 00:44:02,097
...three... humped...

763
00:44:14,610 --> 00:44:16,237
- Here...
- Yeah. Hurry.

764
00:44:16,403 --> 00:44:18,447
- ...you...
- Thank you. "2-9-T-H-D-O-3."

765
00:44:18,614 --> 00:44:19,531
...go.

766
00:44:19,782 --> 00:44:22,076
It's registered to Tundratown Limo Service.

767
00:44:22,243 --> 00:44:25,412
A limo took Otterton,
and the limo's in Tundratown!

768
00:44:25,579 --> 00:44:27,331
Way to hustle, bud.
I love you. I owe you.

769
00:44:27,456 --> 00:44:29,291
Hurry! We gotta beat
the rush hour, and...

770
00:44:29,458 --> 00:44:31,418
It's night?

771
00:44:32,503 --> 00:44:34,213
Closed. Great.

772
00:44:34,463 --> 00:44:38,300
And I will bet you
you don't have a warrant to get in.

773
00:44:38,467 --> 00:44:40,344
Darn it. It's a bummer.

774
00:44:40,511 --> 00:44:42,429
You wasted the day on purpose.

775
00:44:42,596 --> 00:44:44,306
Madam, I have a fake badge.

776
00:44:44,390 --> 00:44:46,809
I would never impede
your pretend investigation.

777
00:44:46,976 --> 00:44:52,106
It's not a pretend investigation. Look,
see? See him? This otter is missing.

778
00:44:52,606 --> 00:44:54,984
Then they should've gotten
a real cop to find him.

779
00:44:55,192 --> 00:44:56,360
What is your problem?

780
00:44:56,527 --> 00:44:58,407
Does seeing me fail
somehow make you feel better

781
00:44:58,487 --> 00:45:00,322
about your own sad, miserable life?

782
00:45:00,489 --> 00:45:02,533
It does. 100%.

783
00:45:02,741 --> 00:45:05,953
Now, since you're sans warrant...

784
00:45:06,120 --> 00:45:08,956
I guess we're... done?

785
00:45:10,624 --> 00:45:13,669
Fine. We are done.
Here's your pen.

786
00:45:15,045 --> 00:45:15,845
Hey!

787
00:45:19,550 --> 00:45:21,093
First off, you throw like a bunny.

788
00:45:21,260 --> 00:45:24,763
Second, you're a very sore loser.
See you later, Officer Fluff.

789
00:45:24,930 --> 00:45:27,725
So sad this is over.
I wish I could've helped more.

790
00:45:27,891 --> 00:45:31,103
The thing is, you don't need a warrant
if you have probable cause...

791
00:45:31,270 --> 00:45:35,190
and I'm pretty sure I saw
a shifty lowlife climbing the fence.

792
00:45:35,399 --> 00:45:37,901
So you're helping plenty. Come on.

793
00:45:40,070 --> 00:45:42,740
2-9-T-H-D-0-3.
This is it!

794
00:45:51,707 --> 00:45:52,833
Polar bear fur.

795
00:45:56,045 --> 00:45:57,463
- Oh, my God!
- What?

796
00:45:57,796 --> 00:46:00,382
The Velvety Pipes of Jerry Vole.

797
00:46:01,467 --> 00:46:02,593
But on CD.

798
00:46:02,760 --> 00:46:04,136
Who still uses CDs?

799
00:46:10,184 --> 00:46:14,480
Carrots, if your otter was here,
he had a very bad day.

800
00:46:16,106 --> 00:46:18,442
Those are claw marks.

801
00:46:19,151 --> 00:46:20,819
You ever seen anything like this?

802
00:46:20,986 --> 00:46:22,029
No.

803
00:46:22,571 --> 00:46:23,947
Wait, look.

804
00:46:26,158 --> 00:46:28,327
This is him, Emmitt Otterton.

805
00:46:28,494 --> 00:46:30,871
He was definitely here.

806
00:46:31,038 --> 00:46:33,207
What do you think happened?

807
00:46:35,918 --> 00:46:37,086
Now, wait a minute.

808
00:46:37,836 --> 00:46:41,840
Polar bear fur, Rat Pack music, fancy cup?

809
00:46:42,007 --> 00:46:43,567
I know whose car this is. We got to go.

810
00:46:43,801 --> 00:46:44,760
Why? Whose car is it?

811
00:46:44,843 --> 00:46:47,846
The most feared crime-boss
in Tundratown. They call him Mr. Big.

812
00:46:48,013 --> 00:46:50,474
And he does not like me.
So we gotta go!

813
00:46:50,641 --> 00:46:52,434
I'm not leaving.
This is a crime scene.

814
00:46:52,643 --> 00:46:54,937
It's gonna be an even bigger
crime scene if Mr. Big finds me here...

815
00:46:55,145 --> 00:46:56,438
so we're leaving right now.

816
00:46:58,065 --> 00:46:59,066
Raymond!

817
00:46:59,233 --> 00:47:01,276
And is that Kevin?
Long time, no see.

818
00:47:01,860 --> 00:47:06,532
And, speaking of "no see,"
how about you forget you saw me?

819
00:47:06,698 --> 00:47:07,741
For old times' sake?

820
00:47:07,991 --> 00:47:08,951
That's a no.

821
00:47:22,923 --> 00:47:25,175
What did you do that made
Mr. Big so mad at you?

822
00:47:26,218 --> 00:47:28,971
I may have sold him
a very expensive wool rug...

823
00:47:29,138 --> 00:47:33,016
that was made from the fur of a skunk...

824
00:47:33,183 --> 00:47:35,185
...'s butt.

825
00:47:36,186 --> 00:47:38,480
Sweet cheese and crackers.

826
00:47:53,245 --> 00:47:54,621
Is that Mr. Big?

827
00:47:54,830 --> 00:47:55,831
No.

828
00:47:55,998 --> 00:47:57,166
What about him?

829
00:47:57,332 --> 00:47:58,500
- Is that him?
- No!

830
00:48:01,545 --> 00:48:02,546
That's got to be him.

831
00:48:02,713 --> 00:48:04,353
Stop talking, stop talking, stop talking!

832
00:48:13,557 --> 00:48:15,642
Mr. Big, sir. This is a simple...

833
00:48:17,352 --> 00:48:18,395
Yeah.

834
00:48:20,898 --> 00:48:22,608
This is a simple misunderstanding.

835
00:48:24,067 --> 00:48:27,529
You come here, unannounced, on the
day my daughter is to be married.

836
00:48:27,696 --> 00:48:30,056
Well, actually, we were brought here
against our will, so...

837
00:48:31,450 --> 00:48:33,660
The point is, I did not know
that it was your car...

838
00:48:33,827 --> 00:48:36,330
and I certainly did not know
about your daughter's wedding.

839
00:48:37,748 --> 00:48:39,458
I trusted you, Nicky.

840
00:48:39,583 --> 00:48:41,502
I welcomed you into my home.

841
00:48:41,668 --> 00:48:43,921
We broke bread together.

842
00:48:44,505 --> 00:48:46,548
Gram-mama made you a cannoli.

843
00:48:46,715 --> 00:48:48,842
And how did you repay my generosity?

844
00:48:49,009 --> 00:48:52,971
With a rug made from the butt of a skunk.

845
00:48:53,138 --> 00:48:54,932
A skunk-butt rug.

846
00:48:55,098 --> 00:48:56,642
You disrespected me.

847
00:48:56,808 --> 00:49:01,563
You disrespected my Gram-mama,
who I buried in that skunk butt rug.

848
00:49:03,106 --> 00:49:06,818
I told you never to show your face
here again but here you are...

849
00:49:06,985 --> 00:49:09,905
snooping around with this...

850
00:49:10,072 --> 00:49:11,365
What are you? A performer?

851
00:49:11,532 --> 00:49:12,699
What's with the costume?

852
00:49:12,824 --> 00:49:13,659
Sir. I am a...

853
00:49:13,825 --> 00:49:15,327
Mime! She is a mime.

854
00:49:15,494 --> 00:49:18,664
This... mime cannot speak.
You can't speak if you're a mime.

855
00:49:18,830 --> 00:49:20,082
No, I am a cop.

856
00:49:20,249 --> 00:49:21,875
And I'm on the Emmitt Otterton case.

857
00:49:22,042 --> 00:49:24,586
And my evidence puts him in your car.

858
00:49:24,753 --> 00:49:26,296
So intimidate me all you want.

859
00:49:26,463 --> 00:49:31,218
I'm going to find out what you did
to that otter if it's the last thing I do.

860
00:49:33,262 --> 00:49:36,139
Then I have only one request.

861
00:49:36,306 --> 00:49:39,726
Say hello to Gram-mama.
Ice 'em.

862
00:49:41,353 --> 00:49:42,813
I didn't see nothing!
I'm not saying nothing!

863
00:49:43,021 --> 00:49:44,231
And you never will.

864
00:49:44,398 --> 00:49:45,816
Please! No, no, no!

865
00:49:46,066 --> 00:49:48,569
If you're mad at me about the rug,
I've got more rugs!

866
00:49:48,777 --> 00:49:52,739
Daddy! It's time for our dance!

867
00:49:54,157 --> 00:49:55,450
What did we say?

868
00:49:55,659 --> 00:49:57,703
No icing anyone at my wedding!

869
00:49:57,869 --> 00:50:00,330
I have to, baby. Daddy has to.

870
00:50:00,497 --> 00:50:01,415
Ice 'em.

871
00:50:01,582 --> 00:50:02,457
No, no no!

872
00:50:02,624 --> 00:50:03,584
Wait. Please!

873
00:50:04,084 --> 00:50:07,129
She's the bunny that saved my life yesterday!

874
00:50:07,296 --> 00:50:08,547
From that giant donut.

875
00:50:08,714 --> 00:50:09,715
This bunny?

876
00:50:09,881 --> 00:50:11,550
Yeah! Hi!

877
00:50:11,758 --> 00:50:14,136
Hi. I love your dress.

878
00:50:14,761 --> 00:50:15,846
Thank you.

879
00:50:16,346 --> 00:50:17,347
Put 'em down.

880
00:50:21,184 --> 00:50:22,561
You've done me a great service.

881
00:50:22,728 --> 00:50:24,896
I will help you find the otter.

882
00:50:25,063 --> 00:50:29,985
I will take your kindness and pay it forward.

883
00:50:57,971 --> 00:50:59,681
Otterton is my florist.

884
00:51:00,223 --> 00:51:02,601
He's like a part of the family.

885
00:51:02,809 --> 00:51:05,729
He had something important
he wanted to discuss.

886
00:51:05,896 --> 00:51:08,815
That's why I sent that car to pick him up.

887
00:51:09,524 --> 00:51:11,276
But he never arrived.

888
00:51:11,652 --> 00:51:13,195
Because he was attacked.

889
00:51:13,362 --> 00:51:15,864
No. He attacked.

890
00:51:16,365 --> 00:51:17,449
Otterton?

891
00:51:17,658 --> 00:51:20,744
Otterton. He went crazy.

892
00:51:20,911 --> 00:51:24,873
Ripped up the car,
scared my driver half to death...

893
00:51:25,040 --> 00:51:27,542
and disappeared into the night.

894
00:51:27,709 --> 00:51:30,837
But he's a sweet little otter.

895
00:51:31,880 --> 00:51:36,218
My child, we may be evolved, but deep down...

896
00:51:36,343 --> 00:51:38,595
we are still animals.

897
00:51:40,722 --> 00:51:43,850
You want to find Otterton,
talk to the driver of that car.

898
00:51:43,934 --> 00:51:45,519
His name is Manchas...

899
00:51:45,727 --> 00:51:48,230
lives in the Rainforest District.

900
00:51:48,397 --> 00:51:50,899
Only he can tell you more.

901
00:51:54,695 --> 00:51:56,363
Mr. Manchas?

902
00:51:56,530 --> 00:51:58,573
Judy Hopps, ZPD.

903
00:51:58,782 --> 00:52:01,785
We just wanna know
what happened to Emmitt Otterton.

904
00:52:06,623 --> 00:52:07,708
You...

905
00:52:07,874 --> 00:52:09,084
should be asking...

906
00:52:10,043 --> 00:52:12,045
what happened to me.

907
00:52:13,213 --> 00:52:16,174
A teensy otter did that?

908
00:52:16,341 --> 00:52:17,634
What happened?

909
00:52:17,843 --> 00:52:19,636
He was an animal.

910
00:52:21,054 --> 00:52:21,972
Down...

911
00:52:22,347 --> 00:52:24,599
on all fours.

912
00:52:27,644 --> 00:52:29,062
He was a savage!

913
00:52:39,823 --> 00:52:40,991
There was no warning.

914
00:52:41,199 --> 00:52:43,410
He just kept yelling about the Night Howlers.

915
00:52:44,244 --> 00:52:47,122
Over and over. The Night Howlers.

916
00:52:47,289 --> 00:52:50,667
So you know about the Night Howlers, too?

917
00:52:50,917 --> 00:52:52,252
Good. Good, good.

918
00:52:52,461 --> 00:52:57,007
Because the Night Howlers are exactly
what we are here to talk about.

919
00:52:57,174 --> 00:52:58,550
Right?
- Yup.

920
00:52:59,342 --> 00:53:01,428
So, you just open the door...

921
00:53:01,595 --> 00:53:03,180
and tell us what you know...

922
00:53:03,346 --> 00:53:05,474
and we will tell you
what we know. Okay?

923
00:53:07,142 --> 00:53:09,144
Okay.

924
00:53:10,187 --> 00:53:12,147
Clever fox.

925
00:53:15,859 --> 00:53:17,986
Mr. Manchas?

926
00:53:22,324 --> 00:53:23,158
Buddy?

927
00:53:23,325 --> 00:53:24,451
Are you...

928
00:53:24,576 --> 00:53:25,452
okay?

929
00:53:28,497 --> 00:53:29,998
Run. Run!

930
00:53:34,961 --> 00:53:36,087
What is wrong with him?

931
00:53:36,254 --> 00:53:37,589
I don't know!

932
00:53:42,552 --> 00:53:43,512
Jump!

933
00:53:46,181 --> 00:53:47,182
Come on!

934
00:53:52,145 --> 00:53:53,271
Head down!

935
00:53:55,273 --> 00:53:56,733
Officer Hopps to Dispatch.

936
00:53:56,900 --> 00:54:00,195
Are you familiar with Gazelle, greatest
singer of our lifetime, angel with horns?

937
00:54:00,362 --> 00:54:03,448
Okay, hold on. Keep watching.
Who's that beside her? Who is it?

938
00:54:03,532 --> 00:54:06,827
Wow, you are one hot dancer,
Benjamin Clawhauser.

939
00:54:08,453 --> 00:54:11,915
It's me! Did you think it was real?
It looks so real!

940
00:54:12,082 --> 00:54:14,751
It's not, it's just a new app.
Hold on a second.

941
00:54:14,918 --> 00:54:16,378
Clawhauser!

942
00:54:16,545 --> 00:54:20,423
Listen to me, we have a 10-91!
Jaguar gone savage!

943
00:54:20,549 --> 00:54:21,508
Vine and Tuh-junja!

944
00:54:21,675 --> 00:54:22,843
It's "Tuh-hunga!"

945
00:54:23,426 --> 00:54:25,011
Okay, we 're sending backup! Hopps?

946
00:54:25,470 --> 00:54:26,930
Hopps!

947
00:54:28,598 --> 00:54:29,398
There!

948
00:54:29,516 --> 00:54:31,017
Head for the sky trams!

949
00:54:40,360 --> 00:54:41,987
Get in! Carrots.

950
00:54:42,153 --> 00:54:43,113
Carrots!

951
00:54:43,280 --> 00:54:44,114
Go!

952
00:54:45,532 --> 00:54:47,367
No, no, no!

953
00:54:47,534 --> 00:54:48,785
Buddy.

954
00:54:48,952 --> 00:54:50,745
One predator to another...

955
00:54:55,083 --> 00:54:56,877
Now, I can tell you're a little tense...

956
00:54:57,043 --> 00:54:59,043
so I'm just gonna give you
a little personal space!

957
00:55:05,427 --> 00:55:07,387
Rabbit, whatever you do, do not let go!

958
00:55:08,722 --> 00:55:10,515
- I'm gonna let go!
- No, you, what?

959
00:55:10,599 --> 00:55:12,601
One, two...

960
00:55:19,441 --> 00:55:22,110
Carrots. You saved my life.

961
00:55:22,277 --> 00:55:25,488
Well. That's what we do at the ZPD.

962
00:55:42,589 --> 00:55:44,633
Well, this should be good.

963
00:55:46,468 --> 00:55:47,761
I thought this was
just a missing mammal case...

964
00:55:47,928 --> 00:55:49,429
but it's way bigger.

965
00:55:49,596 --> 00:55:51,723
Mr. Otterton did not just disappear.

966
00:55:51,890 --> 00:55:55,936
I believe he and this jaguar,
they went savage, sir.

967
00:55:56,102 --> 00:56:00,982
Savage? This isn't the Stone Age,
Hopps. Animals don't "go savage."

968
00:56:01,066 --> 00:56:04,152
I thought so, too, until I saw this.

969
00:56:06,279 --> 00:56:09,074
What? He was right here.

970
00:56:09,157 --> 00:56:10,700
The "savage" jaguar?

971
00:56:10,825 --> 00:56:13,745
Sir, I know what I saw.
He almost killed us!

972
00:56:13,828 --> 00:56:19,334
Or, maybe any aggressive predator
looks savage to you rabbits.

973
00:56:19,417 --> 00:56:20,335
Let's go!

974
00:56:20,502 --> 00:56:21,503
Wait, sir.

975
00:56:21,670 --> 00:56:23,588
I'm not the only one who saw him.

976
00:56:23,672 --> 00:56:24,923
Nick!

977
00:56:25,632 --> 00:56:27,550
You think I'm gonna believe a fox?

978
00:56:27,676 --> 00:56:29,427
He was a key witness, and I...

979
00:56:29,511 --> 00:56:31,763
Two days to find the otter...

980
00:56:31,846 --> 00:56:34,182
or you quit.

981
00:56:34,307 --> 00:56:36,059
That was the deal.

982
00:56:36,184 --> 00:56:37,727
Badge.

983
00:56:43,191 --> 00:56:44,985
- But, sir, we...
- Badge!

984
00:56:50,490 --> 00:56:51,950
No.

985
00:56:52,826 --> 00:56:54,494
What did you say, fox?

986
00:56:54,577 --> 00:56:58,123
Sorry. What I said was, "no."

987
00:56:58,206 --> 00:57:01,042
She will not be giving you that badge.

988
00:57:01,167 --> 00:57:04,921
Look, you give her a clown vest
and a three-wheeled joke-mobile...

989
00:57:05,046 --> 00:57:07,966
and two days to solve a case
you guys haven't cracked in two weeks?

990
00:57:08,842 --> 00:57:09,926
Yeah.

991
00:57:10,051 --> 00:57:11,553
No wonder she needed to get help from a fox.

992
00:57:11,720 --> 00:57:13,888
None of you guys
were gonna help her, were you?

993
00:57:14,055 --> 00:57:15,390
Here's the thing, Chief.

994
00:57:15,515 --> 00:57:16,975
You gave her the 48 hours...

995
00:57:17,058 --> 00:57:19,060
so, technically, we still have...

996
00:57:19,227 --> 00:57:21,354
10 left to find our Mr. Otterton...

997
00:57:21,521 --> 00:57:23,857
and that is exactly what we're gonna do.

998
00:57:24,024 --> 00:57:28,361
So, if you'll excuse us, we have a very
big lead to follow and a case to crack.

999
00:57:28,528 --> 00:57:29,863
Good day.

1000
00:57:35,618 --> 00:57:37,162
Officer Hopps.

1001
00:57:59,726 --> 00:58:01,269
Thank you.

1002
00:58:02,353 --> 00:58:04,773
Never let them see that they get to you.

1003
00:58:06,024 --> 00:58:07,275
So...

1004
00:58:07,442 --> 00:58:09,861
things do get to you?

1005
00:58:10,028 --> 00:58:12,447
No, I mean, not anymore...

1006
00:58:12,614 --> 00:58:16,618
but I was small and emotionally
unbalanced like you once.

1007
00:58:16,785 --> 00:58:17,744
Har-har..

1008
00:58:17,911 --> 00:58:19,871
No, it's true.

1009
00:58:20,038 --> 00:58:23,124
I think I was 8, or maybe 9...

1010
00:58:23,291 --> 00:58:28,296
and all I wanted to do
was join the Junior Ranger Scouts.

1011
00:58:28,379 --> 00:58:31,174
So, my mom scraped together enough money

1012
00:58:31,257 --> 00:58:33,635
to buy me a brand-new uniform...

1013
00:58:33,802 --> 00:58:36,346
because, by God, I was gonna fit in.

1014
00:58:36,513 --> 00:58:39,891
Even if I was the only predator in the troop.

1015
00:58:39,974 --> 00:58:40,892
The only fox.

1016
00:58:41,059 --> 00:58:41,893
Okay, Nick.

1017
00:58:42,060 --> 00:58:43,645
I was gonna be pan' of a pack.

1018
00:58:43,770 --> 00:58:45,271
Ready for initiation?

1019
00:58:45,355 --> 00:58:47,232
Yeah. Pretty much born ready.

1020
00:58:47,315 --> 00:58:49,067
I was so proud.

1021
00:58:51,986 --> 00:58:52,821
Okay.

1022
00:58:52,946 --> 00:58:55,406
Now, raise your right paw
and deliver the oath.

1023
00:58:55,907 --> 00:58:58,993
I, Nicholas Wilde, promise to be brave...

1024
00:58:59,160 --> 00:59:02,080
loyal, helpful, and trustworthy.

1025
00:59:03,289 --> 00:59:06,084
Even though you're a fox?

1026
00:59:06,167 --> 00:59:07,502
What?

1027
00:59:10,004 --> 00:59:12,340
No! What did I do wrong, you guys?

1028
00:59:12,465 --> 00:59:14,676
No, please! Tell me!
What did I do wrong?

1029
00:59:14,801 --> 00:59:16,427
What did I do?

1030
00:59:18,304 --> 00:59:20,849
If you thought we would ever
trust a fox without a muzzle...

1031
00:59:21,266 --> 00:59:23,935
you're even dumber than you look!

1032
00:59:25,270 --> 00:59:28,064
Aw, is he gonna cry?

1033
00:59:43,746 --> 00:59:46,249
I learned two things that day.

1034
00:59:46,416 --> 00:59:47,709
One...

1035
00:59:47,876 --> 00:59:51,921
I was never gonna let anyone see
that they got to me.

1036
00:59:52,714 --> 00:59:55,216
And two?

1037
00:59:55,341 --> 01:00:00,346
If the world's only gonna see a fox
as shifty and untrustworthy...

1038
01:00:00,513 --> 01:00:03,516
there's no point in trying
to be anything else.

1039
01:00:03,683 --> 01:00:07,228
Nick, you are so much more than that.

1040
01:00:11,399 --> 01:00:13,026
Boy, look at that traffic down there.

1041
01:00:13,193 --> 01:00:14,861
How about we go out to Chuck
in Traffic Central.

1042
01:00:15,028 --> 01:00:16,868
Chuck, how are things looking on the Jam Cams?

1043
01:00:17,906 --> 01:00:20,033
Nick, I'm glad you told me.

1044
01:00:20,992 --> 01:00:22,493
The Jam Cams.

1045
01:00:22,660 --> 01:00:25,330
- Seriously. It's okay.
- No, no, no.

1046
01:00:25,496 --> 01:00:28,666
There are traffic cameras everywhere!

1047
01:00:28,833 --> 01:00:30,543
All over the canopy!

1048
01:00:30,627 --> 01:00:32,027
Whatever happened to that jaguar...

1049
01:00:32,086 --> 01:00:33,296
The traffic cams would have caught it!

1050
01:00:33,463 --> 01:00:34,297
Bingo!

1051
01:00:34,839 --> 01:00:36,299
Pretty sneaky, slick!

1052
01:00:36,466 --> 01:00:39,802
However, if you didn't have access
to the system before...

1053
01:00:39,969 --> 01:00:43,598
I doubt Chief Buffalo Butt
is gonna let you into it now.

1054
01:00:43,765 --> 01:00:44,933
No.

1055
01:00:45,808 --> 01:00:48,102
But I have a friend at City Hall who might.

1056
01:00:48,978 --> 01:00:51,689
Sir, if we could just review
these very important...

1057
01:00:52,315 --> 01:00:53,115
Sir...

1058
01:00:53,233 --> 01:00:54,609
I'm so sorry. Sir!

1059
01:00:54,734 --> 01:00:57,779
Okay. I heard you, Bellwether.
Just take care of it.

1060
01:00:58,446 --> 01:01:00,823
Please. And clear my afternoon.
I'm going out.

1061
01:01:00,990 --> 01:01:03,470
No, no! But, sir, you do have a meeting
with Herds and Grazing.

1062
01:01:03,576 --> 01:01:04,953
Sir, if I could just...

1063
01:01:06,663 --> 01:01:08,623
Oh, mutton chops.

1064
01:01:10,375 --> 01:01:11,834
Assistant Mayor Bellwether?

1065
01:01:12,001 --> 01:01:13,211
We need your help.

1066
01:01:14,712 --> 01:01:16,672
We just need to get into
the traffic cam database.

1067
01:01:25,515 --> 01:01:27,100
So fluffy!

1068
01:01:27,267 --> 01:01:28,067
Hey!

1069
01:01:28,184 --> 01:01:29,644
Sheep never let me get this close.

1070
01:01:29,811 --> 01:01:31,251
You can't just touch a sheep's wool!

1071
01:01:31,354 --> 01:01:32,355
It's like cotton candy.

1072
01:01:32,522 --> 01:01:33,481
Stop it!

1073
01:01:33,648 --> 01:01:34,732
Where to?

1074
01:01:35,191 --> 01:01:38,152
Rainforest District, Vine and Tujunga.

1075
01:01:38,444 --> 01:01:40,238
There! Traffic cams for the whole city.

1076
01:01:40,405 --> 01:01:42,615
This is so exciting, actually!

1077
01:01:42,782 --> 01:01:44,462
I never get to do anything this important.

1078
01:01:44,575 --> 01:01:46,953
But you're the Assistant Mayor of Zootopia.

1079
01:01:47,120 --> 01:01:49,497
I'm more of a glorified secretary.

1080
01:01:49,664 --> 01:01:52,125
I think Mayor Lionheart
just wanted the sheep vote.

1081
01:01:52,292 --> 01:01:53,626
But he did give me that nice mug.

1082
01:01:55,837 --> 01:01:57,255
Feels good to be appreciated.

1083
01:01:57,422 --> 01:01:58,715
Smell-wether!

1084
01:01:58,881 --> 01:02:01,592
That's a fun little name he likes to use.

1085
01:02:01,759 --> 01:02:03,636
I called him Lion-fart once.

1086
01:02:03,803 --> 01:02:06,222
He did not care for that. Let me tell you,
it was not a good day for me.

1087
01:02:06,389 --> 01:02:07,189
Yes, sir?

1088
01:02:07,307 --> 01:02:08,933
I thought you were going to
cancel my afternoon!

1089
01:02:09,183 --> 01:02:10,977
Oh, dear. I better go.

1090
01:02:11,102 --> 01:02:14,731
Let me know what you find.
It was really nice for me to be...

1091
01:02:14,897 --> 01:02:16,816
While we 're young, Smell-wether!

1092
01:02:19,152 --> 01:02:20,945
You think when she goes to sleep
she counts herself?

1093
01:02:21,112 --> 01:02:22,989
Shush. Okay, traffic cams.

1094
01:02:23,239 --> 01:02:26,284
Tujunga, Tujunga... We're in.

1095
01:02:31,873 --> 01:02:33,666
Who are these guys?

1096
01:02:34,834 --> 01:02:37,670
Timberwolves.
Look at these dumb-dumbs.

1097
01:02:42,300 --> 01:02:44,927
Bet you a nickel one of them is gonna howl.

1098
01:02:45,053 --> 01:02:46,387
And there it is.

1099
01:02:46,554 --> 01:02:49,307
What is it with wolves and the howling?
It's like...

1100
01:02:49,390 --> 01:02:51,309
Howlers. Night Howlers.

1101
01:02:51,476 --> 01:02:53,644
That's what Manchas was afraid of!
Wolves!

1102
01:02:53,811 --> 01:02:56,272
The wolves are the Night Howlers.
If they took Manchas...

1103
01:02:56,439 --> 01:02:58,149
I'll bet they took Otterton, too.

1104
01:02:58,316 --> 01:03:00,860
All we gotta do is find out where they went.

1105
01:03:08,409 --> 01:03:09,369
Wait, where did they go?

1106
01:03:09,452 --> 01:03:12,663
If I wanted to avoid surveillance
because I was doing something illegal...

1107
01:03:12,830 --> 01:03:13,790
which I never have...

1108
01:03:13,956 --> 01:03:15,750
I would use the maintenance tunnel 6B.

1109
01:03:16,459 --> 01:03:17,710
Which would put them out...

1110
01:03:17,835 --> 01:03:19,921
right there.

1111
01:03:20,046 --> 01:03:23,633
Well, look at you, Junior Detective.

1112
01:03:23,716 --> 01:03:27,929
You know, I think you'd actually
make a pretty good cop.

1113
01:03:28,096 --> 01:03:29,347
How dare you.

1114
01:03:30,098 --> 01:03:31,307
Acacia Alley?

1115
01:03:31,474 --> 01:03:32,809
Ficus Underpass.

1116
01:03:32,934 --> 01:03:34,185
South Canyon.

1117
01:03:34,268 --> 01:03:35,388
They're heading out of town.

1118
01:03:35,978 --> 01:03:37,563
Where does that road go?

1119
01:04:22,150 --> 01:04:23,317
Gary, quit it!

1120
01:04:23,443 --> 01:04:24,483
You're gonna start a howl.

1121
01:04:24,527 --> 01:04:25,528
I didn't start it!

1122
01:04:32,994 --> 01:04:34,454
Come on.

1123
01:04:42,253 --> 01:04:44,881
You are a clever bunny.

1124
01:05:00,646 --> 01:05:03,858
It looks like this was a hospital.

1125
01:05:16,871 --> 01:05:18,789
You know, after you.
You're the cop.

1126
01:05:27,256 --> 01:05:29,425
Okay, all clear.

1127
01:05:34,388 --> 01:05:36,766
All this equipment is brand new.

1128
01:05:38,476 --> 01:05:39,477
Carrots.

1129
01:05:42,146 --> 01:05:43,189
Claw marks.

1130
01:05:43,314 --> 01:05:45,733
Yeah, huge claw marks.
What kind of...

1131
01:06:05,336 --> 01:06:07,255
Mr. Manchas.

1132
01:06:17,098 --> 01:06:18,599
It's him.

1133
01:06:18,766 --> 01:06:20,768
We found our otter.

1134
01:06:20,935 --> 01:06:22,520
Mr. Otterton.

1135
01:06:22,728 --> 01:06:26,065
My name is Officer Judy Hopps.
Your wife sent me to find you.

1136
01:06:26,232 --> 01:06:28,025
We're gonna get you out of here now.

1137
01:06:29,318 --> 01:06:30,570
Or not.

1138
01:06:30,736 --> 01:06:32,989
Guess he's in no rush
to get home to the missus.

1139
01:06:33,155 --> 01:06:35,408
11,12,13,14...

1140
01:06:35,491 --> 01:06:38,703
Not including Manchas, it's 14.

1141
01:06:38,786 --> 01:06:41,664
Chief Bogo handed out 14 missing mammal files.

1142
01:06:41,747 --> 01:06:42,790
They're all here.

1143
01:06:42,873 --> 01:06:46,627
All the missing mammals are right here.

1144
01:06:51,841 --> 01:06:54,760
Enough! I don't want excuses, Doctor!

1145
01:06:54,885 --> 01:06:55,761
I want answers.

1146
01:06:55,928 --> 01:06:57,221
Mayor Lionheart, please.

1147
01:06:57,305 --> 01:06:59,223
We're doing everything we can.

1148
01:06:59,307 --> 01:07:00,224
Really?

1149
01:07:00,308 --> 01:07:02,226
Because I got a dozen and a half animals here

1150
01:07:02,310 --> 01:07:04,061
who have gone off-the-rails crazy...

1151
01:07:04,228 --> 01:07:05,771
and you can't tell me why.

1152
01:07:05,896 --> 01:07:08,524
Now, I'd call that awfully far
from "doing everything."

1153
01:07:08,691 --> 01:07:09,900
Sir...

1154
01:07:10,026 --> 01:07:11,777
it may be time to consider their biology.

1155
01:07:11,944 --> 01:07:13,821
What? What do you mean "biology?"

1156
01:07:13,946 --> 01:07:16,824
The only animals going savage are predators.

1157
01:07:16,949 --> 01:07:19,619
We cannot keep it a secret.
We need to come forward.

1158
01:07:20,911 --> 01:07:22,622
Great idea. Tell the public.

1159
01:07:22,788 --> 01:07:25,750
And how do you think they're gonna feel
about their mayor...

1160
01:07:25,916 --> 01:07:27,585
who is a lion?

1161
01:07:27,668 --> 01:07:28,836
I'll be ruined!

1162
01:07:28,919 --> 01:07:30,212
Well, what does Chief Bogo say?

1163
01:07:30,379 --> 01:07:32,506
Chief Bogo doesn't know.

1164
01:07:32,673 --> 01:07:35,926
And we are going to keep it that way.

1165
01:07:36,719 --> 01:07:37,553
No, no, no!

1166
01:07:37,762 --> 01:07:39,013
Someone's here.

1167
01:07:39,180 --> 01:07:40,598
Sir, you need to go, now!

1168
01:07:40,765 --> 01:07:42,433
Security! Sweep the area!

1169
01:07:50,566 --> 01:07:52,360
Great! We're dead. We're dead.

1170
01:07:52,485 --> 01:07:54,820
That's it. I'm dead, you're dead.
Everybody's dead!

1171
01:07:54,945 --> 01:07:56,030
Can you swim?

1172
01:07:56,197 --> 01:07:59,033
What? Can I swim?
Yes, I can swim. Why?

1173
01:08:19,011 --> 01:08:22,890
Carrots! Hopps! Judy!

1174
01:08:23,599 --> 01:08:25,309
We gotta tell Bogo!

1175
01:08:26,477 --> 01:08:30,898
Wow! You are one hot dancer,
Chief Bogo.

1176
01:08:33,442 --> 01:08:35,236
- Chief Bogo!
- Not now!

1177
01:08:35,319 --> 01:08:36,320
Wait, is that Gazelle?

1178
01:08:36,445 --> 01:08:37,245
No.

1179
01:08:37,405 --> 01:08:40,241
I'm Gazelle, and you are one hot dancer.

1180
01:08:40,366 --> 01:08:42,076
You have the app too?

1181
01:08:43,494 --> 01:08:44,620
Chief!

1182
01:08:44,787 --> 01:08:47,456
Clawhauser, can't you see I'm working
on the missing mammal cases?

1183
01:08:47,623 --> 01:08:49,904
Yes of course! About that, sir.
Officer Hopps just called.

1184
01:08:51,377 --> 01:08:52,420
She found all of them.

1185
01:08:53,212 --> 01:08:55,256
Wow! I'm impressed.

1186
01:08:58,426 --> 01:09:01,095
Mayor Lionheart, you have the right
to remain silent. Anything...

1187
01:09:01,262 --> 01:09:03,931
You don't understand.
I was trying to protect the city!

1188
01:09:04,056 --> 01:09:06,142
You were just trying to protect your job.

1189
01:09:06,308 --> 01:09:09,145
No. Listen, we still don't know
why this is happening.

1190
01:09:09,311 --> 01:09:11,605
It could destroy Zootopia.

1191
01:09:11,689 --> 01:09:12,857
You have the right to remain silent.

1192
01:09:13,023 --> 01:09:15,703
Anything you say can and will
be used against you in a court of law.

1193
01:09:20,990 --> 01:09:22,241
Ladies and gentlemammals.

1194
01:09:23,367 --> 01:09:25,202
14 mammals went missing...

1195
01:09:25,453 --> 01:09:29,039
and all 14 have been found
by our newest recruit...

1196
01:09:29,206 --> 01:09:30,566
who will speak to you in a moment.

1197
01:09:30,666 --> 01:09:31,959
But first, let me remind you...

1198
01:09:32,042 --> 01:09:33,127
I'm so nervous.

1199
01:09:33,294 --> 01:09:35,087
Okay. Press Conference 101.

1200
01:09:35,254 --> 01:09:37,798
You wanna look smart? Answer
their question with your own question...

1201
01:09:37,965 --> 01:09:41,010
and then answer
that question. Like this.

1202
01:09:41,177 --> 01:09:44,513
"Excuse me. Officer Hopps,
what can you tell us about the case?"

1203
01:09:44,680 --> 01:09:46,265
"Well, was this a tough case?

1204
01:09:46,432 --> 01:09:48,559
"Yes. Yes, it was."
You see?

1205
01:09:48,726 --> 01:09:50,394
You should be there with me.

1206
01:09:50,561 --> 01:09:52,021
We did this together.

1207
01:09:52,146 --> 01:09:55,232
Well, am I a cop?
No. No, I am not.

1208
01:09:55,816 --> 01:09:59,945
Funny you should say that,
because I've been thinking...

1209
01:10:00,154 --> 01:10:04,658
It would be nice to have a partner.

1210
01:10:09,205 --> 01:10:12,958
Here. In case
you need something to write with.

1211
01:10:14,752 --> 01:10:17,713
At twenty-two hundred hours,
we found all these missing animals...

1212
01:10:17,880 --> 01:10:20,466
Officer Hopps, it's time.

1213
01:10:20,633 --> 01:10:24,303
They appear to be in good health,
physically, if not emotionally.

1214
01:10:24,386 --> 01:10:29,350
So now, I'll turn things over
to the officer who cracked the case.

1215
01:10:29,642 --> 01:10:31,227
Officer Judy Hopps.

1216
01:10:32,895 --> 01:10:34,855
- Officer Hopps!
- Officer Hopps!

1217
01:10:34,980 --> 01:10:35,898
Over here!

1218
01:10:36,065 --> 01:10:36,899
Yes?

1219
01:10:37,066 --> 01:10:38,859
What can you tell us about
the animals that went savage?

1220
01:10:39,026 --> 01:10:43,531
Well, the animals in question...

1221
01:10:46,492 --> 01:10:48,661
Are they all different species?

1222
01:10:48,828 --> 01:10:50,830
Yes. Yes, they are.

1223
01:10:51,956 --> 01:10:54,291
Okay, so what is the connection?

1224
01:10:54,375 --> 01:10:58,170
All we know is that they are all
members of the predator family.

1225
01:10:58,254 --> 01:11:01,382
So, predators are the only ones going savage?

1226
01:11:01,465 --> 01:11:04,218
That is accurate. Yes.

1227
01:11:04,343 --> 01:11:05,719
Why is this happening?

1228
01:11:05,845 --> 01:11:06,971
We still don't know.

1229
01:11:09,807 --> 01:11:13,561
It may have something to do with biology.

1230
01:11:13,644 --> 01:11:15,020
What do you mean by that?

1231
01:11:15,104 --> 01:11:17,398
A biological component.

1232
01:11:17,481 --> 01:11:20,693
You know, something in their DNA.

1233
01:11:21,360 --> 01:11:23,362
In their DNA?
Can you elaborate on that, please?

1234
01:11:23,445 --> 01:11:27,741
Yes, what I mean is, thousands of years ago...

1235
01:11:29,410 --> 01:11:34,206
predators survived through their aggressive...

1236
01:11:34,290 --> 01:11:35,958
hunting instincts.

1237
01:11:36,083 --> 01:11:37,084
For whatever reason...

1238
01:11:37,209 --> 01:11:39,689
they seem to be reverting back
to their primitive, savage ways.

1239
01:11:40,004 --> 01:11:41,463
Of course they did.

1240
01:11:44,258 --> 01:11:45,676
Aw, is he gonna cry?

1241
01:11:45,759 --> 01:11:47,887
Officer Hopps, could it happen again?

1242
01:11:47,970 --> 01:11:51,348
It is possible.
So we must be vigilant.

1243
01:11:51,432 --> 01:11:54,768
And we at the ZPD are prepared
and are here to protect you.

1244
01:11:54,852 --> 01:11:56,353
Will more mammals go savage?

1245
01:11:56,437 --> 01:11:57,757
What is being done to protect us?

1246
01:11:57,771 --> 01:12:00,190
Have you considered a mandatory
quarantine on predators?

1247
01:12:00,274 --> 01:12:03,527
Okay, thank you, Officer Hopps.
That's all the time that we have.

1248
01:12:03,611 --> 01:12:04,904
No more questions.

1249
01:12:07,448 --> 01:12:08,490
Was I okay?

1250
01:12:08,657 --> 01:12:10,951
You did fine.

1251
01:12:11,118 --> 01:12:12,703
That went so fast.

1252
01:12:12,786 --> 01:12:15,372
I didn't get a chance to mention you,
or say anything about how we...

1253
01:12:15,456 --> 01:12:18,042
I think you said plenty.

1254
01:12:18,125 --> 01:12:19,835
What do you mean?

1255
01:12:19,960 --> 01:12:22,338
"Clearly there's
a biological component?"

1256
01:12:22,504 --> 01:12:27,343
"These predators may be reverting back
to their primitive, savage ways?"

1257
01:12:27,509 --> 01:12:29,136
Are you serious?

1258
01:12:29,345 --> 01:12:31,805
I just stated the facts of the case.

1259
01:12:31,972 --> 01:12:34,475
I mean it's not like a bunny could go savage.

1260
01:12:34,600 --> 01:12:36,477
Right. But a fox could?

1261
01:12:36,644 --> 01:12:38,979
Nick, stop it.
You're not like them.

1262
01:12:39,146 --> 01:12:40,898
Oh, there's a "them" now?

1263
01:12:41,065 --> 01:12:43,734
You know what I mean.
You're not that kind of predator.

1264
01:12:43,901 --> 01:12:45,361
The kind that needs to be muzzled?

1265
01:12:45,527 --> 01:12:49,531
The kind that makes you think
you need to carry around fox repellent?

1266
01:12:49,698 --> 01:12:53,535
Yeah, don't think I didn't notice
that little item the first time we met.

1267
01:12:53,702 --> 01:12:56,830
So let me ask you a question.
Are you afraid of me?

1268
01:12:58,415 --> 01:13:00,834
Do you think I might go nuts?

1269
01:13:01,335 --> 01:13:03,170
Do you think I might go savage?

1270
01:13:03,337 --> 01:13:05,422
Do you think I might try to...

1271
01:13:05,589 --> 01:13:06,590
eat you?

1272
01:13:10,219 --> 01:13:11,762
I knew it.

1273
01:13:14,098 --> 01:13:16,350
Just when I thought somebody
actually believed in me.

1274
01:13:16,517 --> 01:13:20,270
Probably best if you don't have
a predator as a partner.

1275
01:13:26,110 --> 01:13:28,195
No. Nick. Nick!

1276
01:13:28,362 --> 01:13:30,364
Officer Hopps! Were you just
threatened by that predator?

1277
01:13:30,531 --> 01:13:32,199
No, he's my friend.

1278
01:13:32,324 --> 01:13:34,159
We can't even trust our own friends?

1279
01:13:34,368 --> 01:13:36,203
That is not what I said! Please!

1280
01:13:36,370 --> 01:13:37,170
Are we safe?

1281
01:13:37,204 --> 01:13:39,039
Have any foxes gone savage?

1282
01:13:39,456 --> 01:13:41,750
More bad news in this city gripped by fear.

1283
01:13:41,834 --> 01:13:44,962
A caribou is in critical condition,
the victim of a mauling...

1284
01:13:45,129 --> 01:13:47,131
by a savage polar bear.

1285
01:13:47,256 --> 01:13:49,633
This, the 27th such attack,
comes just one week...

1286
01:13:49,717 --> 01:13:53,178
after ZPD Officer Judy Hopps
connected the violence

1287
01:13:53,262 --> 01:13:55,931
to traditionally predatory animals.

1288
01:13:56,140 --> 01:13:59,810
Meanwhile, a peace rally
organized by pop star Gazelle...

1289
01:13:59,935 --> 01:14:01,395
was marred by protest.

1290
01:14:01,478 --> 01:14:02,938
Go back to the forest, predator!

1291
01:14:03,063 --> 01:14:05,065
I'm from the savanna!

1292
01:14:05,149 --> 01:14:07,026
Zootopia is a unique place.

1293
01:14:07,192 --> 01:14:10,654
It's a crazy, beautiful, diverse city...

1294
01:14:10,821 --> 01:14:13,198
where we celebrate our differences.

1295
01:14:13,365 --> 01:14:14,783
This is not the Zootopia I know.

1296
01:14:16,326 --> 01:14:19,413
The Zootopia I know is better than this.

1297
01:14:19,580 --> 01:14:21,790
We don't just blindly assign blame.

1298
01:14:26,962 --> 01:14:29,256
We don't know why these attacks
keep happening...

1299
01:14:30,132 --> 01:14:34,261
but it is irresponsible
to label all predators as savages.

1300
01:14:40,684 --> 01:14:42,770
That's not my Emmitt.

1301
01:14:45,230 --> 01:14:47,691
We cannot let fear divide us.

1302
01:14:47,858 --> 01:14:52,404
Please give me back the Zootopia I love.

1303
01:14:55,574 --> 01:14:56,950
Come on, Hopps.

1304
01:14:57,117 --> 01:14:58,494
The new mayor wants to see us.

1305
01:14:58,660 --> 01:15:00,954
The mayor? Why?

1306
01:15:01,121 --> 01:15:04,083
It would seem you've arrived.

1307
01:15:07,294 --> 01:15:08,962
Clawhauser?

1308
01:15:09,129 --> 01:15:10,672
What are you doing?

1309
01:15:12,466 --> 01:15:14,343
They thought it would be better...

1310
01:15:14,510 --> 01:15:19,264
if a predator such as myself
wasn't the first face that you see...

1311
01:15:19,431 --> 01:15:21,600
when you walk into the ZPD.

1312
01:15:21,892 --> 01:15:23,018
What?

1313
01:15:24,061 --> 01:15:26,522
They're gonna move me to records.
It's downstairs.

1314
01:15:27,189 --> 01:15:29,149
It's by the boiler.

1315
01:15:31,276 --> 01:15:32,778
Hopps!

1316
01:15:39,701 --> 01:15:41,161
I don't understand.

1317
01:15:41,328 --> 01:15:44,331
Our city is 90% prey, Judy...

1318
01:15:44,498 --> 01:15:47,000
and right now they're just really scared.

1319
01:15:47,709 --> 01:15:50,671
You're a hero to them.
They trust you.

1320
01:15:50,838 --> 01:15:57,177
And so that's why Chief Bogo and I want
you to be the public face of the ZPD.

1321
01:16:05,686 --> 01:16:07,354
I'm not a hero.

1322
01:16:08,105 --> 01:16:11,567
I came here
to make the world a better place...

1323
01:16:11,733 --> 01:16:13,277
but I think I broke it.

1324
01:16:13,443 --> 01:16:17,614
Don't give yourself so much credit, Hopps.

1325
01:16:17,781 --> 01:16:21,201
The world has always been broken,
that's why we need good cops.

1326
01:16:22,703 --> 01:16:23,871
Like you.

1327
01:16:25,455 --> 01:16:30,419
With all due respect, sir, a good cop...

1328
01:16:30,627 --> 01:16:33,881
is supposed to serve and protect.

1329
01:16:34,047 --> 01:16:36,341
Help the city.

1330
01:16:36,592 --> 01:16:38,969
Not tear it apart.

1331
01:16:42,431 --> 01:16:44,933
I don't deserve this badge.

1332
01:16:45,058 --> 01:16:45,893
Hopps...

1333
01:16:46,018 --> 01:16:49,229
Judy, you've worked so hard to get here.

1334
01:16:49,313 --> 01:16:51,940
It's what you wanted since you were a kid.

1335
01:16:52,065 --> 01:16:53,775
You can't quit.

1336
01:16:53,942 --> 01:16:55,944
Thank you for the opportunity.

1337
01:17:15,422 --> 01:17:16,882
A dozen carrots.

1338
01:17:17,049 --> 01:17:18,175
- Thanks.
- Have a nice day.

1339
01:17:18,342 --> 01:17:19,343
Come on.

1340
01:17:25,807 --> 01:17:28,018
Hey, there, Jude.
Jude the dude.

1341
01:17:28,185 --> 01:17:30,938
Remember that one?
How we doing?

1342
01:17:31,104 --> 01:17:32,105
I'm fine.

1343
01:17:32,272 --> 01:17:34,733
You are not fine.
Your ears are droopy.

1344
01:17:36,026 --> 01:17:38,403
Why did I think I could make a difference?

1345
01:17:38,528 --> 01:17:40,155
Because you're a trier, that's why.

1346
01:17:40,322 --> 01:17:41,949
You've always been a trier.

1347
01:17:42,115 --> 01:17:43,575
Oh, I tried.

1348
01:17:43,700 --> 01:17:47,537
And it made life so much
worse for so many innocent predators.

1349
01:17:48,288 --> 01:17:50,008
Not all of them, though.
Speak of the devil.

1350
01:17:50,082 --> 01:17:51,333
Right on time.

1351
01:17:53,377 --> 01:17:56,171
Is that Gideon Grey?

1352
01:17:56,338 --> 01:17:58,340
Yep. It sure is.
We work with him now.

1353
01:17:58,507 --> 01:17:59,549
He's our partner.

1354
01:17:59,716 --> 01:18:01,718
And we would never have considered it
had you not opened our minds.

1355
01:18:01,885 --> 01:18:02,886
That's right.

1356
01:18:03,053 --> 01:18:05,613
Gid's turned into one of the
top pastry chefs in the tri-burrows.

1357
01:18:10,018 --> 01:18:12,729
That's really cool, you guys.

1358
01:18:13,230 --> 01:18:14,564
Gideon Grey.

1359
01:18:15,023 --> 01:18:16,149
I'll be darned.

1360
01:18:16,316 --> 01:18:20,779
Hey, Judy. I'd just like to say I'm sorry
for the way I behaved in my youth.

1361
01:18:20,946 --> 01:18:22,739
I had a lot of self-doubt,

1362
01:18:22,823 --> 01:18:26,451
and it manifested itself in the form
of unchecked rage and aggression.

1363
01:18:26,618 --> 01:18:28,745
I was a major jerk.

1364
01:18:29,788 --> 01:18:33,333
I know a thing or two about being a jerk.

1365
01:18:34,418 --> 01:18:37,087
Anyhow, I brought y'all these pies.

1366
01:18:38,463 --> 01:18:41,925
Hey, kids, don't you run through
that midnicampum holicithias.

1367
01:18:42,884 --> 01:18:44,720
Well, now, there's a $4 word, Mr. H.

1368
01:18:44,886 --> 01:18:47,514
My family always
just called them Night Howlers.

1369
01:18:49,182 --> 01:18:50,475
What did you say?

1370
01:18:50,642 --> 01:18:52,242
Gid's talking about those flowers, Judy.

1371
01:18:52,519 --> 01:18:54,146
I use them to keep the bugs off the produce...

1372
01:18:54,313 --> 01:18:55,897
but I don't like the little ones
going near 'em...

1373
01:18:56,064 --> 01:18:57,441
on account of what happened
to your uncle Terry.

1374
01:18:57,607 --> 01:18:59,443
Yeah, Terry ate one whole when we were kids

1375
01:18:59,526 --> 01:19:00,777
and went completely nuts.

1376
01:19:00,944 --> 01:19:03,238
He bit the dickens out of your mother.

1377
01:19:03,405 --> 01:19:05,741
A bunny can go savage.

1378
01:19:05,907 --> 01:19:09,202
Savage? Well, that's a strong word.
But it did hurt like the devil.

1379
01:19:09,369 --> 01:19:13,415
Sure it did! There's a sizable divot
in your arm. I'd call that savage.

1380
01:19:13,582 --> 01:19:16,918
Night Howlers aren't wolves, they're flowers!

1381
01:19:17,085 --> 01:19:20,130
The flowers are making
the predators go savage!

1382
01:19:20,297 --> 01:19:22,299
That's it! That's what I've been missing!

1383
01:19:22,924 --> 01:19:23,967
Keys!

1384
01:19:24,092 --> 01:19:25,372
Keys, keys, keys!
Hurry! Come on!

1385
01:19:26,303 --> 01:19:28,055
Thank you! I love you! Bye!

1386
01:19:33,560 --> 01:19:34,644
You catch any of that, Bon?

1387
01:19:34,811 --> 01:19:35,854
Not one bit.

1388
01:19:36,021 --> 01:19:39,691
That makes me feel a little bit better.
I thought she was talking in tongues.

1389
01:19:57,000 --> 01:19:58,794
Who is it?

1390
01:19:59,086 --> 01:20:00,003
I need to find Nick.

1391
01:20:00,170 --> 01:20:01,588
Please.

1392
01:20:02,798 --> 01:20:03,840
Nick?

1393
01:20:04,591 --> 01:20:05,926
Nick!

1394
01:20:08,637 --> 01:20:10,263
Oh, Nick.

1395
01:20:10,389 --> 01:20:12,182
Night Howlers aren't wolves.

1396
01:20:12,349 --> 01:20:14,059
They're toxic flowers.

1397
01:20:14,684 --> 01:20:16,269
I think someone
is targeting predators on purpose

1398
01:20:16,353 --> 01:20:18,855
and making them go savage!

1399
01:20:19,231 --> 01:20:20,524
Wow.

1400
01:20:21,775 --> 01:20:23,652
Isn't that interesting?

1401
01:20:26,154 --> 01:20:30,033
Wait! I know you'll never forgive me!
And I don't blame you.

1402
01:20:30,951 --> 01:20:33,954
I wouldn't forgive me either.
I was ignorant...

1403
01:20:34,955 --> 01:20:38,625
And irresponsible and small-minded.

1404
01:20:40,085 --> 01:20:42,629
But predators shouldn't have
to suffer for my mistakes.

1405
01:20:43,547 --> 01:20:45,549
I have to fix this.

1406
01:20:46,007 --> 01:20:48,385
But I can't do it without you.

1407
01:20:50,762 --> 01:20:53,432
And after we're done...

1408
01:20:53,598 --> 01:20:55,308
you can hate me.

1409
01:20:56,351 --> 01:20:57,978
And that'll be fine.

1410
01:20:58,145 --> 01:21:01,440
Because I was a horrible friend...

1411
01:21:01,606 --> 01:21:02,983
and I hurt you.

1412
01:21:03,150 --> 01:21:09,656
And you can walk away knowing
that you were right all along.

1413
01:21:09,823 --> 01:21:13,785
I really am just a dumb bunny.

1414
01:21:17,747 --> 01:21:21,376
I really am just a dumb bunny.

1415
01:21:23,044 --> 01:21:26,339
I really am just a dumb bunny.

1416
01:21:26,506 --> 01:21:29,426
Don't worry, Carrots.
I'll let you erase it...

1417
01:21:29,593 --> 01:21:30,594
in 48 hours.

1418
01:21:33,513 --> 01:21:36,016
All right. Get in here.

1419
01:21:37,851 --> 01:21:41,938
Okay. You bunnies are so emotional.

1420
01:21:42,230 --> 01:21:44,524
There we go. Deep breath.

1421
01:21:44,691 --> 01:21:48,361
Are you just trying to steal the pen?
Is that what this is?

1422
01:21:49,362 --> 01:21:51,364
You are standing on my tail, though.
Off, off, off.

1423
01:21:51,448 --> 01:21:52,282
I'm sorry.

1424
01:21:57,370 --> 01:21:59,873
I thought you guys only grew carrots.

1425
01:22:00,749 --> 01:22:01,750
What's the plan?

1426
01:22:02,000 --> 01:22:03,627
We are gonna follow the Night Howlers.

1427
01:22:03,793 --> 01:22:05,003
Okay. How?

1428
01:22:05,212 --> 01:22:06,505
Know this guy?

1429
01:22:07,380 --> 01:22:09,132
I told you. I know everybody.

1430
01:22:10,550 --> 01:22:13,595
Step right up.
Anything you need, I got it.

1431
01:22:13,887 --> 01:22:14,887
All your favorite movies!

1432
01:22:14,930 --> 01:22:17,098
I got movies that
haven't even been released yet.

1433
01:22:18,558 --> 01:22:21,603
Hey, 15% off! 20!
Make me an offer! Come on!

1434
01:22:21,770 --> 01:22:25,565
Well, well, look who it is.
The duke of bootleg.

1435
01:22:25,815 --> 01:22:26,775
What's it to you, Wilde?

1436
01:22:26,900 --> 01:22:28,276
Shouldn't you be melting down
a Popsicle or something?

1437
01:22:28,443 --> 01:22:29,803
Hey, if it ain't Flopsy the Copsy.

1438
01:22:30,904 --> 01:22:34,157
We both know those weren't
moldy onions I caught you stealing.

1439
01:22:34,324 --> 01:22:36,644
What were you gonna do
with those Night Howlers, Wezzleton?

1440
01:22:36,743 --> 01:22:38,662
It's Weaselton! Duke Weaselton!

1441
01:22:38,828 --> 01:22:40,664
And I ain't talking, rabbit.

1442
01:22:40,830 --> 01:22:42,958
And there ain't nothing you can do to make me.

1443
01:22:44,543 --> 01:22:45,544
Ice him.

1444
01:22:48,755 --> 01:22:51,299
You dirty rat! Why are you helping her?
She's a cop!

1445
01:22:51,466 --> 01:22:54,469
And the godmother to my future granddaughter.

1446
01:22:54,636 --> 01:22:56,763
I'm gonna name her Judy.

1447
01:22:58,932 --> 01:23:00,100
Ice this weasel.

1448
01:23:00,267 --> 01:23:02,394
All right, all right, please!
I'll talk! I'll talk.

1449
01:23:02,561 --> 01:23:04,062
I stole them Night Howlers
so I could sell 'em.

1450
01:23:04,145 --> 01:23:06,064
They offered me what I couldn't refuse.

1451
01:23:06,231 --> 01:23:07,031
Money.

1452
01:23:07,274 --> 01:23:09,109
And to whom did you sell them?

1453
01:23:09,276 --> 01:23:12,279
A ram named Doug.
We got a drop spot underground.

1454
01:23:12,445 --> 01:23:15,448
Just watch it.
Doug is the opposite of friendly.

1455
01:23:16,449 --> 01:23:17,951
He's unfriendly.

1456
01:23:28,128 --> 01:23:29,379
Come on.

1457
01:23:42,350 --> 01:23:44,144
The weasel wasn't lying.

1458
01:23:44,311 --> 01:23:47,314
Yeah, looks like old Doug's cornered
the market on Night Howlers.

1459
01:24:31,232 --> 01:24:32,150
You got Doug here.

1460
01:24:32,984 --> 01:24:35,028
What's the mark?

1461
01:24:35,195 --> 01:24:39,366
Cheetah in Sahara Square. Got it.

1462
01:24:39,532 --> 01:24:41,201
Serious? Yeah, I know they're fast.

1463
01:24:41,368 --> 01:24:42,452
I can hit him.

1464
01:24:42,661 --> 01:24:45,622
Listen, I hit a tiny little otter through
the open window of a moving car.

1465
01:25:00,637 --> 01:25:02,681
Yeah, I'll buzz you when it's done.

1466
01:25:02,847 --> 01:25:05,287
Or you'll see it on the news.
You know, whichever comes first.

1467
01:25:06,226 --> 01:25:09,396
Hey, Doug, open up!
We've got your latte.

1468
01:25:09,521 --> 01:25:12,399
Woolter and Jesse are back,
so I'm leaving now.

1469
01:25:12,607 --> 01:25:13,650
Out.

1470
01:25:13,733 --> 01:25:14,984
Where are you going?
Get back here!

1471
01:25:15,068 --> 01:25:17,320
What are you doing?
He's gonna see you!

1472
01:25:17,445 --> 01:25:18,947
What are you looking at? Hey!

1473
01:25:19,072 --> 01:25:21,658
Whatever you're thinking,
stop thinking it. Carrots!

1474
01:25:22,701 --> 01:25:23,702
Carrots!

1475
01:25:23,868 --> 01:25:26,162
It better have the extra foam this time.

1476
01:25:29,332 --> 01:25:30,333
Hey, open UP!

1477
01:25:30,500 --> 01:25:32,919
What are you doing?
You just trapped us in here.

1478
01:25:33,086 --> 01:25:34,686
We need to get this evidence to the ZPD!

1479
01:25:34,921 --> 01:25:36,840
Great! Here it is. Got it!

1480
01:25:37,006 --> 01:25:38,925
- No. All of it!
- Wait, what?

1481
01:25:42,971 --> 01:25:45,432
Great, you're a conductor now?

1482
01:25:45,598 --> 01:25:48,184
It would take a miracle
to get this rust-bucket going.

1483
01:25:50,145 --> 01:25:52,105
Well. Hallelujah.

1484
01:25:52,272 --> 01:25:55,066
We kinda got a situation at the lab.

1485
01:25:55,150 --> 01:25:56,276
It just got worse!

1486
01:25:58,153 --> 01:25:59,279
Mission accomplished.

1487
01:25:59,362 --> 01:26:01,802
Would it be premature
for me to do a little victory toot-toot?

1488
01:26:01,948 --> 01:26:04,451
All right. One toot-toot.

1489
01:26:06,911 --> 01:26:09,372
Well, I can cross that off the bucket list.

1490
01:26:20,175 --> 01:26:22,802
I may have to rescind that victory
toot-toot.

1491
01:26:24,554 --> 01:26:25,930
Maybe that's just hail.

1492
01:26:26,765 --> 01:26:27,599
Come here!

1493
01:26:29,976 --> 01:26:31,686
Back off!

1494
01:26:36,191 --> 01:26:37,650
Incoming!

1495
01:26:40,195 --> 01:26:40,995
Carrots!

1496
01:26:41,029 --> 01:26:42,489
Don't stop! Keep going!

1497
01:26:42,655 --> 01:26:43,990
No, no! Please stop!

1498
01:26:44,199 --> 01:26:45,825
Do not stop this car!

1499
01:27:07,931 --> 01:27:09,641
Speed up, Nick! Speed up!

1500
01:27:09,849 --> 01:27:11,142
There's another train coming!

1501
01:27:11,309 --> 01:27:12,769
Trust me. Speed up!

1502
01:27:17,023 --> 01:27:18,817
Stop the train!

1503
01:27:19,776 --> 01:27:20,777
Hey!

1504
01:27:21,069 --> 01:27:22,737
Need some help?

1505
01:27:26,407 --> 01:27:27,742
Oh, no, no, no! Too fast!

1506
01:27:27,909 --> 01:27:29,702
Hold on!

1507
01:27:41,172 --> 01:27:42,799
I think this is our stop!

1508
01:27:51,891 --> 01:27:54,352
Maybe some of the evidence survived.

1509
01:28:03,903 --> 01:28:05,488
Everything is gone.

1510
01:28:05,655 --> 01:28:07,907
We've lost it all.

1511
01:28:08,032 --> 01:28:09,075
Yeah...

1512
01:28:09,200 --> 01:28:10,577
except for this.

1513
01:28:12,328 --> 01:28:13,872
Nick! Yes!

1514
01:28:13,955 --> 01:28:16,165
Come on! We gotta get to the ZPD.

1515
01:28:16,791 --> 01:28:19,002
Cut through the Natural History Museum!

1516
01:28:30,763 --> 01:28:31,723
There it is!

1517
01:28:31,890 --> 01:28:33,391
Judy!

1518
01:28:33,516 --> 01:28:35,101
Judy!

1519
01:28:35,268 --> 01:28:37,103
Mayor Bellwether!

1520
01:28:37,270 --> 01:28:38,470
We found out what's happening.

1521
01:28:38,521 --> 01:28:40,356
Someone's darting predators with a serum.

1522
01:28:40,523 --> 01:28:42,442
That's what's making them go savage.

1523
01:28:42,609 --> 01:28:45,445
I'm so proud of you, Judy.
You did just a super job!

1524
01:28:45,612 --> 01:28:47,280
Thank you, ma'am.

1525
01:28:51,409 --> 01:28:53,244
How did you know where to find us?

1526
01:28:53,411 --> 01:28:55,246
I'll go ahead and I'll take that case now.

1527
01:28:55,413 --> 01:29:00,418
You know what? I think Nick and I
will just take this to the ZPD.

1528
01:29:04,964 --> 01:29:05,965
Run.

1529
01:29:07,175 --> 01:29:08,551
Get them.

1530
01:29:14,557 --> 01:29:15,357
Carrots!

1531
01:29:17,310 --> 01:29:19,312
I got you! Come here, come here.

1532
01:29:21,564 --> 01:29:23,650
Okay, now just relax.

1533
01:29:24,984 --> 01:29:25,985
Blueberry?

1534
01:29:26,152 --> 01:29:27,236
Pass.

1535
01:29:28,988 --> 01:29:30,990
Come on out, Judy.

1536
01:29:31,199 --> 01:29:32,450
Take the case.

1537
01:29:32,659 --> 01:29:33,826
Get it to Bogo.

1538
01:29:33,993 --> 01:29:35,620
I'm not gonna leave you behind.
That's not happening.

1539
01:29:35,787 --> 01:29:37,163
I can't walk.

1540
01:29:37,330 --> 01:29:38,331
We'll think of something.

1541
01:29:38,498 --> 01:29:40,833
We're on the same team, Judy.

1542
01:29:41,000 --> 01:29:44,879
Underestimated, underappreciated.

1543
01:29:45,088 --> 01:29:47,173
Aren't you sick of it?

1544
01:29:50,009 --> 01:29:53,805
Predators.
They may be strong and loud...

1545
01:29:53,972 --> 01:29:57,558
but prey outnumber predators 10 to 1.

1546
01:30:01,145 --> 01:30:02,188
Think Of it.

1547
01:30:02,397 --> 01:30:04,357
90% of the population...

1548
01:30:04,524 --> 01:30:06,776
united against a common enemy.

1549
01:30:06,901 --> 01:30:09,362
We'll be unstoppable.

1550
01:30:15,451 --> 01:30:16,869
Over there!

1551
01:30:20,790 --> 01:30:21,791
Help!

1552
01:30:33,553 --> 01:30:36,305
Well, you should have just stayed
on the carrot farm, huh?

1553
01:30:36,472 --> 01:30:39,017
It really is too bad. I did like you.

1554
01:30:39,183 --> 01:30:41,728
What are you going to do? Kill me?

1555
01:30:42,812 --> 01:30:44,731
No, of course not.

1556
01:30:44,897 --> 01:30:45,898
He is.

1557
01:30:46,733 --> 01:30:49,193
No! Nick!

1558
01:30:49,944 --> 01:30:54,323
Yes, police! There's a savage fox
in the Natural History Museum.

1559
01:30:54,490 --> 01:30:56,576
Officer Hopps is down! Please, hurry.

1560
01:30:56,701 --> 01:30:59,078
No. Nick, don't do this. Fight it.

1561
01:31:00,079 --> 01:31:02,331
But he can't help it, can he?

1562
01:31:02,540 --> 01:31:07,003
Since preds are just biologically
predisposed to be savages.

1563
01:31:18,389 --> 01:31:20,767
Gosh. Think of the headline!

1564
01:31:20,933 --> 01:31:25,104
"Hero cop killed by savage fox."

1565
01:31:26,272 --> 01:31:29,942
So that's it? Prey fears predator,
and you stay in power?

1566
01:31:30,109 --> 01:31:31,611
Yeah, pretty much.

1567
01:31:31,778 --> 01:31:32,820
It won't work!

1568
01:31:32,987 --> 01:31:35,573
Fear always works.

1569
01:31:35,740 --> 01:31:38,785
And I'll dart every predator
in Zootopia to keep it that way.

1570
01:31:41,913 --> 01:31:43,039
Oh, Nick.

1571
01:31:43,623 --> 01:31:44,749
No.

1572
01:31:47,335 --> 01:31:48,628
BYE-bye, bunny.

1573
01:31:55,259 --> 01:31:59,472
Blood! Blood! Blood and death.

1574
01:31:59,639 --> 01:32:00,890
All right, you're milking it.

1575
01:32:01,140 --> 01:32:02,308
Besides, I think we got it.

1576
01:32:02,475 --> 01:32:04,727
We got it up there, thank you,
Yakety-yak!

1577
01:32:04,811 --> 01:32:06,687
You laid it all out beautifully.

1578
01:32:06,813 --> 01:32:07,647
What?

1579
01:32:07,814 --> 01:32:09,014
Are you looking for the serum?

1580
01:32:09,690 --> 01:32:10,775
Well, it's right here.

1581
01:32:10,942 --> 01:32:11,943
What you've got in the weapon there?

1582
01:32:12,110 --> 01:32:15,196
Those are blueberries.
From my family's farm.

1583
01:32:15,321 --> 01:32:17,740
They are delicious.
You should try some.

1584
01:32:18,866 --> 01:32:21,452
I framed Lionheart.
I can frame you, too.

1585
01:32:21,619 --> 01:32:23,830
It's my word against yours.

1586
01:32:24,831 --> 01:32:26,165
Actually...

1587
01:32:26,332 --> 01:32:30,002
And I'll dart every predator in Zootopia
to keep it that way.

1588
01:32:30,169 --> 01:32:31,921
...it's your word against yours.

1589
01:32:32,046 --> 01:32:35,174
It's called a hustle, sweetheart. Boom.

1590
01:32:43,683 --> 01:32:45,059
Former mayor Dawn Bellwether
is behind bars today...

1591
01:32:45,685 --> 01:32:47,436
guilty of masterminding the savage attacks

1592
01:32:47,520 --> 01:32:49,647
that have plagued Zootopia of late.

1593
01:32:49,814 --> 01:32:53,359
<i>Her predecessor, Leodore Lionheart,
denies any knowledge of her plot...</i>

1594
01:32:53,526 --> 01:32:55,862
claiming he was just trying
to protect the city.

1595
01:32:56,070 --> 01:32:58,489
Did I falsely imprison those animals?

1596
01:32:58,656 --> 01:32:59,907
<i>Well, yes. Yes, I did.</i>

1597
01:33:00,074 --> 01:33:03,870
It was a classic "doing the wrong thing
for the right reason" kind of a deal.

1598
01:33:04,036 --> 01:33:06,956
In related news, doctors say
the Night Howler antidote

1599
01:33:07,039 --> 01:33:08,207
is proving effective...

1600
01:33:08,374 --> 01:33:11,210
in rehabilitating the afflicted predators.

1601
01:33:14,213 --> 01:33:15,298
Emmitt.

1602
01:33:17,216 --> 01:33:18,217
Emmitt.

1603
01:33:21,721 --> 01:33:22,763
Thank you.

1604
01:33:25,892 --> 01:33:27,560
When I was a kid...

1605
01:33:27,727 --> 01:33:31,355
...l thought Zootopia
was this perfect place.

1606
01:33:31,522 --> 01:33:35,902
Where everyone got along
and anyone could be anything.

1607
01:33:37,111 --> 01:33:40,198
Turns out, real life's a little bit
more complicated...

1608
01:33:40,364 --> 01:33:43,034
than a slogan on a bumper sticker.

1609
01:33:43,201 --> 01:33:44,869
Real life is messy.

1610
01:33:46,746 --> 01:33:50,291
We all have limitations.
We all make mistakes.

1611
01:33:50,583 --> 01:33:54,921
Which means, hey, glass half-full,
we all have a lot in common.

1612
01:33:55,129 --> 01:33:57,924
And the more we try
to understand one another...

1613
01:33:58,090 --> 01:34:00,968
the more exceptional each of us will be.

1614
01:34:01,969 --> 01:34:03,888
But we have to try.

1615
01:34:05,139 --> 01:34:07,683
So, no matter what type of animal you are...

1616
01:34:07,892 --> 01:34:09,268
from the biggest elephant...

1617
01:34:10,269 --> 01:34:12,939
to our first fox...

1618
01:34:14,190 --> 01:34:18,236
<i>I implore you... try.</i>

1619
01:34:18,361 --> 01:34:21,781
Try to make the world a better place.

1620
01:34:23,449 --> 01:34:24,450
Look inside yourself...

1621
01:34:24,575 --> 01:34:26,285
and recognize that change...

1622
01:34:27,119 --> 01:34:29,080
starts with you.

1623
01:34:30,498 --> 01:34:31,999
It starts with me.

1624
01:34:33,000 --> 01:34:36,295
It starts with all of us.

1625
01:34:44,512 --> 01:34:47,390
All right, enough! Shut it!

1626
01:34:47,640 --> 01:34:51,560
We have some new recruits
with us this morning...

1627
01:34:51,686 --> 01:34:52,853
including our first fox.

1628
01:34:53,646 --> 01:34:54,981
Who cares?

1629
01:34:55,106 --> 01:34:57,746
You should have your own line
of inspirational greeting cards, sir.

1630
01:34:57,817 --> 01:34:58,943
Shut your mouth, Wilde.

1631
01:34:59,068 --> 01:35:00,945
Assignments.

1632
01:35:01,112 --> 01:35:03,656
Officers Grizzoli, Fangmeyer, Delgato...

1633
01:35:03,823 --> 01:35:05,825
Tundratown SWAT.

1634
01:35:05,950 --> 01:35:07,994
Snarlov, Higgins, Wolfard...

1635
01:35:08,160 --> 01:35:10,162
undercover.

1636
01:35:10,329 --> 01:35:12,665
Hopps, Wilde.

1637
01:35:12,790 --> 01:35:14,292
Parking duty.

1638
01:35:14,417 --> 01:35:16,127
Dismissed.

1639
01:35:16,252 --> 01:35:18,170
Just kidding!

1640
01:35:18,337 --> 01:35:21,340
We have reports of a street racer
tearing up Savannah Central.

1641
01:35:22,049 --> 01:35:23,843
Find him, shut him down.

1642
01:35:23,926 --> 01:35:27,847
So, are all rabbits bad drivers,
or is it just you?

1643
01:35:30,141 --> 01:35:31,225
Oops. Sorry.

1644
01:35:33,060 --> 01:35:34,061
Sly bunny.

1645
01:35:34,228 --> 01:35:35,521
Dumb fox.

1646
01:35:35,688 --> 01:35:37,732
You know you love me.

1647
01:35:37,898 --> 01:35:39,525
Do I know that?

1648
01:35:39,692 --> 01:35:41,569
Yes. Yes, I do.

1649
01:35:56,250 --> 01:36:00,212
Sir, you were going 115 miles per hour.
I hope you have a good explanation.

1650
